const SCENARIOS=[{"id": "iran-nuclear", "title": "THE IRAN NUCLEAR DEAL", "subtitle": "2003 — 2018", "tagline": "Sanctions, enrichment, and the most complex arms control agreement since the Cold War", "color": "#e05252", "icon": "☢", "phases": [{"briefing_id": "art-096", "briefing_title": "Iran A geopolitical jigsaw puzzle", "briefing_text": "Iran: A geopolitical jigsaw puzzle Published: Wed, 07/13/2016 - 12:30 Updated: Wed, 07/13/2016 - 12:31 In an interview with Hardnews, Dr Sujata Ashwarya, a prominent academic and noted expert on the Middle East, talks about the various implications of Iran’s rehabilitation in the international pecking order, the impact of the US presidential elections on Iran and the long-term consequences of the Chabahar deal between India and Iran Sanjay Kapoor Delhi How do you see the return of Iran in the international order after the signing of the P5+1 deal? Will it aggravate contestation with Saudi Arabia and Israel? The nuclear deal has reinforced Iran’s capacity to pursue its regional ambitions more aggressively, and even aspire for a global role. Iran’s regional influence has been growing steadily since the Iraq War in 2003. Saddam Hussein’s downfall removed the proverbial ‘gatekeeper of the Arab east against the revolutionary regime in Persia’, to the great trepidation of Saudi Arabia and other Arab Gulf countries. Shia ascendance in Iraq shaped the arc of Iranian influence from Baghdad to Beirut. Iran is assisting Baghdad in taking on the Islamic State (IS), and along with its proxy Hizbullah, Tehran is helping President Assad fight the rebels and the IS in Syria – via logistic support and training as well as supply of weapons. The nuclear deal has also empowered Iran to stretch its sphere of influence and change the situation in Yemen by making the war costly, both in material and human casualties, for Saudi Arabia and its allies. Iran’s assistance to the Houthis (who belong to the Zaydi sect, also known as Fivers, of Islam) in Yemen, right on the Saudi doorstep, brings the Iranian arc of influence full circle, both literally and figuratively. Therefore, normalisation of Iran has come in the wake of unprecedented Iranian influence in the entire region, much to the chagrin of both Saudi Arabia and Israel, who have opposed the nuclear deal. The Saudis are apprehensive of the strategic leverage and latitude it would give to Iranian pursuits, when the tag of a ‘pariah state’ is gone and the dividend from sanctions relief takes effect. Israel, or in any case, the current dispensation there, is convinced that Iran will rescind and cheat somewhere along the line, diluting the Jewish state’s regional military supremacy. In view of Arab failings, starkly evident in the events of the Arab Spring, the P5+1 deal is also indicative of America’s recognition of Iran as a pla", "phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Enrichment Crisis", "period": "2003 – 2006", "context": "Iran's nuclear programme has become a global flashpoint. After secretly developing enrichment capability at Natanz, Iran announces it will resume uranium enrichment, withdrawing from the Paris Agreement. The IAEA demands full access. The US and Israel threaten military action. Iran insists it has a sovereign right to civilian nuclear power.", "question": "Iran has announced it will resume uranium enrichment. The US and Israel are threatening military action. What is the appropriate international response?", "choices": [{"label": "Impose maximum pressure: full sanctions to force capitulation", "outcome_title": "The Hardliners Win", "outcome": "Sanctions bite into Iran's economy — but they harden the Revolutionary Guards and sideline moderates. Iran accelerates enrichment. By 2006, over 3,000 centrifuges are operating. Ahmadinejad is elected and uses Western pressure to consolidate his base.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Offer Iran a diplomatic off-ramp: security guarantees and civilian nuclear assistance", "outcome_title": "A Fragile Opening", "outcome": "The EU-3 opens negotiations. Iran agrees to a temporary suspension. But without direct US involvement, the deal lacks weight. Ahmadinejad's 2005 election changes the calculus. The window narrows — but remains open.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Refer Iran to the UN Security Council and apply multilateral pressure", "outcome_title": "A Diluted Resolution", "outcome": "Russia and China resist comprehensive measures. A diluted resolution passes. Iran dismisses it as political theatre. Enrichment continues, more slowly, but continues.", "score": 0}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-068", "briefing_title": "Iran The Rouhani Checkmate", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Iran: The Rouhani Checkmate Published: Thu, 12/12/2013 - 12:08 Updated: Sun, 02/02/2014 - 19:11 Iran President Hassan Rouhani’s bold move to reach a historic agreement with the United States and the other five powers, opening the way to removing the festering sore of US-Iran enmity, is in line with his personal history of rescuing Iranian diplomacy from crisis with the West Gareth Porter Virginia In a historic deal and a marathon round of negotiations later, Iran entered into an accord with six world powers on a plan of action that seeks to put a long list of “voluntary” limits on its nuclear programme, including disposal of most of the stockpile of 20 percent enriched uranium. The imposition of the current accord stands for six months, ensuring no “further advances” at the Arak heavy water reactor, in return for very limited and reversible lifting of sanctions. The agreement also called for negotiation of a “comprehensive solution” that would involve a “mutually defined enrichment programme” and allow Iran to “fully enjoy its right to nuclear energy for peaceful purposes” under the NPT and would produce a “comprehensive” lifting of all sanctions against Iran on account of its nuclear programme. At first glance, Iran appeared to give up much of its negotiating leverage in the interim agreement by agreeing not just to “freeze” its enrichment programme but to scale back its stockpile of the 20 percent enriched uranium that was the primary proliferation concern of the Obama administration, and by conceding much of the more intrusive International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) monitoring system demanded by the West. But those concessions were “voluntary” and good for six months, so they remained a factor in the broader bargaining dynamics that would be in play in negotiations on a “comprehensive solution”. Iran had made such voluntary concessions in conjunction with the negotiations with the European foreign ministers, and Iran had later withdrawn concessions when they concluded that the Europeans were not acting in good faith. The newly elected President of Iran, Hassan Rouhani, was prepared to do the same if he concluded that the United States and its partners were not negotiating in good faith in the subsequent phase of the negotiations. Despite the substantive negotiating chips that Iran could still play in the talks on a “comprehensive solution”, however, the success of the new sanctions on Iran’s oil and banking sectors in sending Iran’s eco", "phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Rouhani Opening", "period": "2013", "context": "Hassan Rouhani wins the Iranian presidential election on a platform of moderation. For the first time in nearly a decade, Iran has a president willing to negotiate directly with the US. Obama is cautiously optimistic. Israel and Saudi Arabia are furious — they see any deal as legitimising Iran's regional ambitions.", "question": "Rouhani has opened a genuine channel for negotiation. How do you assess this moment?", "choices": [{"label": "This is a genuine opening — pursue aggressive diplomacy immediately", "outcome_title": "The Geneva Agreement", "outcome": "The P5+1 negotiations begin in earnest. The interim Geneva Agreement is signed in November 2013, freezing parts of Iran's programme in exchange for limited sanctions relief. The diplomatic momentum is real.", "score": 2}, {"label": "Rouhani is window dressing — the regime will never compromise", "outcome_title": "The Opportunity Closes", "outcome": "Western scepticism plays directly into the hands of Iranian hardliners. Rouhani is undermined. The Revolutionary Guards consolidate. The chance to forge a deal while a moderate holds power in Tehran is squandered.", "score": -2}, {"label": "Use this moment to press for broader concessions — missiles, regional proxy behaviour", "outcome_title": "Iran Walks Away", "outcome": "Iran refuses to expand the scope of negotiations. Hardliners exploit maximalist demands as proof the West was never serious. A narrower deal becomes harder.", "score": -1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-098", "briefing_title": "‘India can be one of the first countries to benefit from the Iran nuclear deal_", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy ‘India can be one of the first countries to benefit from the Iran nuclear deal' Published: Mon, 08/10/2015 - 11:05 Updated: Wed, 09/16/2015 - 10:56 The Iranian Ambassador to India, Gholamreza Ansari, does not really pull punches when it comes to talking about the role played by Israel in its endeavour to scuttle the nuclear deal. In a freewheeling interview to Hardnews, he says that Israel is desperate to gain acceptance with India and other countries. He does not think that the Islamic State is sectarian as it has killed more Sunnis, whom it claims to represent, than Shias. He is intrigued by the fact that the Islamic State, which seems so dangerous, has never threatened Israel. He feels India could be the first country to benefit once the embargo is lifted from Iran. Excerpts: Sanjay Kapoor & Sadiq Naqvi Delhi How do you perceive the changing equation between the Israelis and the Americans following the Iran nuclear deal? The hostility which is escalating between the Americans and the Israelis is not very simple. It seems that the Americans are feeling that Israel is becoming very costly for them, although they feel that they must support Israel. Maybe there are no doubts in many politicians’ minds in America that they must support Israel, but there are a number of people who are feeling that the support of Israel is becoming very costly, and maybe counterproductive, and this is why Israel is trying to create new political links and revealing their old connections. For example, one is India. They are trying to reveal, ‘Okay, we used to have a very long history of relations, we were very good, and we were very cooperative,’ and so on. It is a show of relations. Same with the Saudis. The Israelis are trying to bring their relations with the Saudis to the forefront now. The way they are inviting people, like they invited Muslims to a seminar or talk in Lucknow and in Delhi. What seminar/talk took place in Delhi? It wasn’t a very well-known institution. The Muslims who participated said it was organised by the Israelis and the Saudis. You know, they are trying to normalise, show some sort of normalisation of Israel’s relations with many countries. They want to say that they are becoming an acceptable country to the public. They are showing ‘how the Saudis are trying to reach us’, ‘how we used to have very long relations with the Indians’, and so on. So it seems that the Israelis are in a very different situation. So they are doing it out of de", "phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The JCPOA", "period": "July 2015", "context": "After twenty months of gruelling negotiations, the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action is signed in Vienna. Iran agrees to limit enrichment to 3.67%, submit to the most intrusive inspections regime in history, and reduce its enriched uranium stockpile by 98%. In return, $150 billion in frozen assets are released. Israel calls it a capitulation. Saudi Arabia is alarmed.", "question": "The JCPOA has been signed. Iran–India trade can resume. But the deal's opponents are mobilising. What happens next?", "choices": [{"label": "Implement fully — integrate Iran into the global economy and strengthen moderates", "outcome_title": "The Deal Holds", "outcome": "Iran's economy grows. Trade expands. Moderates are strengthened. The Chabahar port deal progresses. India moves first among democracies to resume oil purchases. Saudi Arabia, alarmed, escalates proxy conflicts as a counter.", "score": 2}, {"label": "Implement cautiously — wait to see if Iran complies with inspection requirements", "outcome_title": "A Fragile Peace", "outcome": "Slow implementation frustrates Iran. Hardliners argue the West is acting in bad faith. But the deal holds for three years. A tenuous equilibrium.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Demand renegotiation — include Iran's missile programme and regional behaviour", "outcome_title": "Iran Walks Away", "outcome": "Iran refuses. The deal collapses. Hardliners accelerate the enrichment programme. The moment of maximum leverage is sacrificed for an unreachable maximalist position.", "score": -2}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-064", "briefing_title": "After brazen heist targeting Venezuela’s President Maduro, US unlikely to back off on Iran", "briefing_text": "After brazen heist targeting Venezuela’s President Maduro, US unlikely to back off on Iran From the 2009 protests to Starlink-fuelled unrest, myths of digital subversion persist as Iran braces for US pressure, amid growing China–Russia support. Sanjay Kapoor Jan 29  4 min read In 2009, a young official from the US Department of State visited Tehran. At that time, Iran was in ferment. Ahmadinejad was the President of the Islamic nation—a principlist and conservative leader who viscerally hated the US and the West. The young official, Jared Cohen, realized that Iranian youth were managing to evade the religious police, the Basij, and the Pasdaran by using messaging platforms that could not be intercepted by the watchful eyes of those running the surveillance state. Cohen, it is learnt, also discovered that those coordinating the large protests of 2009 were using Twitter (now X). A myth has since been created around the impact of his presence on the protests. As the story goes, the White House cajoled Twitter to postpone its planned maintenance by a few days in order to stoke unrest. It is a different matter that the Iranians soon got a sense of what the Americans were up to and shut down the internet. This tough step taken by the Iranian authorities took the wind out of the protesters’ sails, and the streets quietened down again. The truth discovered later was different. At that time, internet penetration in Iran was low, and public disquiet was fuelled largely by traditional television networks that were not impacted by the shutdown of Twitter. However, the myth of Twitter delaying its maintenance continues to prosper in different variations. Though no one today speaks of the reach of Twitter or X, law enforcement agencies in Iran still believe that much of the mischief infused into the country comes from the internet and its various avatars. This time, the provocation was allegedly triggered by Elon Musk’s Starlink, which enables internet access and satellite phones in different parts of the country. Starlink is resented by authoritarian regimes. In Iran too, it is banned, but those using it to infiltrate social media and spread subversive messages reportedly managed to smuggle it into the country. Iran claims this was an operation perfected by Israel-trained terrorists who first stoked instability and then used Starlink to stay in touch with their terrorist networks. Iranian authorities claim that hundreds of their police personnel were killed by them. Th", "phase_num": 4, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Trump's Withdrawal", "period": "May 2018", "context": "Donald Trump unilaterally withdraws the US from the JCPOA. Despite European attempts to salvage it through INSTEX, US secondary sanctions strangle Iran's economy. Iran begins breaching the deal's limits. By 2021, Iran enriches uranium to 60% — just below weapons-grade.", "question": "The JCPOA is effectively dead. Iran's programme is advancing rapidly. What is the most realistic path forward?", "choices": [{"label": "Revive the JCPOA in modified form — accept some constraints as better than nothing", "outcome_title": "The Endless Negotiation", "outcome": "Biden attempts revival but domestic politics, Israeli pressure, and Iranian hardliners now in power make it very difficult. Talks continue inconclusively. Iran enriches ever closer to weapons-grade.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Accept Iran as a nuclear threshold state — manage through deterrence and integration", "outcome_title": "The New Normal", "outcome": "A fragile new normal emerges. Iran becomes a de facto nuclear threshold state. Saudi Arabia begins its own nuclear programme. A regional arms race, slow-motion, becomes the defining challenge of the next decade.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Military strike on Iranian nuclear facilities", "outcome_title": "Catastrophic Escalation", "outcome": "Even a successful strike delays Iran's programme by 2–3 years. Iran retaliates — Hezbollah attacks Israel, Houthi missiles target Saudi Arabia, the Strait of Hormuz closes. Oil hits $250/barrel. Iran withdraws from the NPT and rebuilds faster, with far more domestic legitimacy.", "score": -3}]}], "outcome_title": "The Unfinished Spiral", "outcome_text": "The JCPOA remains effectively dead. Iran has enriched uranium to near weapons-grade. The Abraham Accords have reshaped Sunni Arab–Israel relations as a counter to Tehran. But no one has found a durable solution: can a revolutionary state with regional hegemonic ambitions and a nuclear programme be integrated into a stable international order? The articles in this archive — from Sanjay Kapoor's geopolitical analyses to Zarif's interview — document this spiral from promise to wreckage."}, {"id": "syria-descent", "title": "SYRIA'S DESCENT", "subtitle": "2011 — 2016", "tagline": "How an Arab Spring became a proxy war that destroyed a country and redrew the map of the Middle East", "color": "#f0883e", "icon": "🔥", "phases": [{"briefing_id": "art-079", "briefing_title": "Exclusive from Syria Here they come, the DOGS OF WAR", "briefing_text": "Exclusive from Syria: Here they come, the DOGS OF WAR Published: Mon, 03/05/2012 - 11:06 Updated: Wed, 04/04/2012 - 10:43 War-mongering in the Middle East is basically driven by Israel's sinister agenda, backed by the West, to remain the region's sole superpower supported by sectarian Arab leaders. Like ravaged Iraq and Libya, where thousands have died, this deadly game can become bloody explosive Sanjay Kapoor Damascus (Syria) Drumbeats of war are on prime time again! There is a feverish edginess in the growing discourse in the western media over how military objectives will be achieved in West Asia. TV images of escalating violence in Syria and nuclear facilities in Iran are feeding into the logic of western intervention. It is no longer a question of 'whether', but of 'when' Iran and Syria would be drawn into the arc of attack. Like it happened first with Iraq, and later with Libya, the western powers are sheathing their diverse agendas in morality to make it look like \"another just war\", so that the colossal loss of life, the pillage and plunder that follow violence, could all be explained away to the gullible – the consumers of mass media, those who consume news like any other well-packaged product – as necessary or unavoidable \"collateral damage\" in the fight for a \"just cause\". However, is this war-mongering for real, or would someone blink to prevent the world from a catastrophe foretold? Will rationality prevail to prevent loss of life and further deepening of the economic crisis, or will the logic of war prevail over the simple desires of ordinary people for peace and stability? Unless someone backs off quickly, the region seems to be perched precariously on the edge of hellfire. Going by the preparations currently on at several levels, it is clear that the stakes are very high for those who are itching for a fight. The coalition led by the US, Europe and Israel, and including some Arab regimes, is attempting to steamroll all opposition by creating a world with no shades of grey. Despite being dumped by history and elections, the neo-conservative bully-logic of 'either you're with us, or you're against us' is once again resonating in the world capitals. It is no longer a question of ‘whether’, but of ‘when’ Iran and Syria would be drawn into the arc of attack India, too, has not been spared this brazen arm-twisting and manipulation. In fact, there has been more of both after it became a temporary member of the Security Council. Its traditional ti", "phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Arab Spring Reaches Damascus", "period": "March 2011", "context": "The Arab Spring has toppled Mubarak in Egypt. Protests erupt in Deraa, Syria. Assad's security forces respond with lethal force — shooting protesters, arresting children. The UN Security Council is deadlocked: Russia and China have vetoed any action.", "question": "Assad's forces have killed hundreds of protesters. The UN is paralysed. What should the international community do?", "choices": [{"label": "Immediate NATO military intervention, modelled on Libya", "outcome_title": "Escalation to Catastrophe", "outcome": "Unlike Libya, Syria has a battle-hardened military, a Russian naval base, and deep Iranian investment. NATO intervention drags the US and Russia toward direct confrontation. Jihadist groups fill the power vacuums instantly.", "score": -2}, {"label": "Support peaceful transition — diplomatic pressure and targeted sanctions", "outcome_title": "Too Little, Too Late", "outcome": "Sanctions hurt ordinary Syrians. Assad has Russian and Iranian lifelines. Gulf states begin funding Islamist factions. The moderate opposition is squeezed out from both sides.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Allow regional and Arab League solutions to emerge", "outcome_title": "The Arab League Fails", "outcome": "Arab League observers are sent, then withdrawn after being shot at. Qatar and Saudi Arabia begin competing over which Syrian rebel groups to arm. Their money flows to the most extreme factions.", "score": -1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-080", "briefing_title": "Syria A pre-meditated escalation by US", "briefing_text": "Syria: A pre-meditated escalation by US Published: Mon, 04/10/2017 - 11:09 Updated: Tue, 08/01/2017 - 12:09 The applecart in the Syrian conflict has once again been upset after the US made its first direct strike against the Assad regime When President Donald Trump ordered the firing of 59 Tomahawk missiles at the airbase that was used by Syrian air force to conduct bombing raids on Islamic State occupied Idlib district, he may have crossed the Rubicon. This decision changes many things-including the balance of power that Russia had created in the region after it thrust itself into the theatre of Syrian war. President Donald Trump is being praised by his former detractors and angrily trashed by his supporters who had cautioned him from being dragged into the conflict by the provocation of a chemical weapons attack on civilians living under the occupation of the Islamic State. At the face of it, Trump was swayed by the horrific images of children gasping for breath under a cloud of poisonous gas and chose to reverse his oft-articulated policy of staying away from Syria. It seems unlikely that the decision was taken in such a great hurry. The US President was struggling to shake off the charge that the Russians helped his election. Former National Security Advisor Michael Flynn’s ignominious exit from the administration due to his links with Moscow and his agreeing to speak out if he was granted immunity from prosecution was making Trump’s presidency look very vulnerable. Besides, his popularity was exploring new depths and he was hurting his cause by engaging in a kind of nepotism not witnessed in the White House in a long time. He appointed his son-in-law Kushner as his advisor to look after key issues including West Asia and his daughter to set up an office in the White House. Ivanka also attends meetings when heads of state come visit the US. According to the grapevine it was Ivanka who persuaded Trump to attack the Al-Shayrat airbase in Syria. She was reportedly horrified by the images that trickled out after the dastardly Sarin gas attack in Khan Sheikhun town, Idlib. In the 100 days that Trump had been in power he had not only failed to get rid of Obamacare, but also his predecessor’s looming ghost in the White House. His attempt to prove that he was wiretapped by the Obama administration was mocked even by the FBI chief. What it really shows is that Trump was desperate for an event to turn around his presidency. And rather he quickly realized that it", "phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Chemical Weapons and the Red Line", "period": "August 2013", "context": "Assad uses sarin nerve agent in Ghouta, killing more than 1,400 civilians. Obama has previously declared this a 'red line'. The British Parliament votes against military action. The American public is war-weary.", "question": "Assad has used sarin gas on civilians. Obama's red line has been crossed. What now?", "choices": [{"label": "Strike Assad's military infrastructure in response", "outcome_title": "A Strike Without a Strategy", "outcome": "US missiles hit Syrian air bases. Assad is weakened but not removed. Russia surges support for Damascus. The war intensifies. Without a plan for what follows, the strikes benefit the most extreme rebel factions.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Accept Russia's offer: Syria surrenders its chemical weapons stockpile", "outcome_title": "The Red Line Erased", "outcome": "Syria's declared stockpile is removed. But Assad retains undeclared capacity and continues using chlorine bombs. The 'red line' becomes a permanent symbol of Western irresolve. ISIS exploits the vacuum.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Arm and train 'moderate' Syrian rebel groups", "outcome_title": "Weapons Without Winners", "outcome": "The CIA programme costs $500 million and produces minimal results. Weapons end up with more radical groups. Meanwhile ISIS takes territory the size of the UK.", "score": -1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-030", "briefing_title": "Russia’s Grand Game in West Asia", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Russia’s Grand Game in West Asia Published: Sat, 11/14/2015 - 09:03 Updated: Sat, 11/14/2015 - 09:08 Moscow has worked to regain political will and military capability and its role in Syria has put it at centrestage alongside the US in the oil-rich region\nLt Gen Rameshwar Yadav Delhi Many strategic commentators argue that Russia’s recent intervention on the side of the Syrian government in its fight against rebel forces and the Islamic State in Syria (ISIS) is a kind of marketing campaign for Russia’s weapons-makers. But while that seems partially true, the Western media has used this pragmatic goal of the Russians to downplay their achievements and obscure their main motivation – which is part of a well-planned ‘Grand Game’ with far-reaching strategic implications for the region and globally. Energy security continues to be the prime reason that developed countries are interested in West Asia. The US has been making political maneuvers in Syria ever since the pro-democracy, anti-Assad protests commenced in 2011 by providing clandestine military support to Syrian rebels (SFA) and also a few jihadi groups such as Al-Nusra. Approximately 10,000 rebel fighters have reportedly been trained and equipped by the CIA since the protests commenced in Syria. On the other hand, Russia is an old supporter of Bashir Assad, as Syria facilitates its access to the Mediterranean, a much-needed toe-hold in West Asia after the loss of Russian influence in Egypt. Without this access to the Mediterranean, Russia would be isolated with no influence in the strategically important junction of Asia, Africa and Europe, and lack access to the Atlantic and Indian Oceans. Accordingly, Russia has high strategic stakes in Syria and has used its veto in the UN in favour of Syria a number of times. Now it has intervened militarily in the face of a dual threat to Assad from the US-backed rebels and the ISIS, who together are in possession of almost 80 per cent of Syrian territory, with one-third occupied by the ISIS. It was the emergence of the predominantly Sunni ISIS in 2014 which prompted a paradigm shift in the politico-military dynamics of the region. It brought in a convergence of interests amongst all the stakeholders, local as well as extra-regional players. While Iraq was the victim, Iran had a strategic opportunity. The Syrian dispensation saw an enhanced threat to its survival, and Saudi Arabia, Turkey and Jordan saw a chance to stir the pot for their own gain. The", "phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Russia Enters", "period": "September 2015", "context": "Russia launches airstrikes in Syria — officially targeting ISIS but primarily striking anti-Assad rebel groups. Iran expands its ground presence through Hezbollah and other proxies. ISIS controls territory the size of the UK. The siege of Aleppo begins.", "question": "Russia and Iran have effectively intervened to save Assad. The geopolitical map is being redrawn by force. How do you read this moment?", "choices": [{"label": "Cooperate with Russia on counter-ISIS operations despite disagreements on Assad", "outcome_title": "The Tactical Alliance", "outcome": "A fragile coalition of necessity forms. ISIS is pushed back. But Russia uses the collaboration to legitimise its broader intervention and Assad's continued rule.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Increase support for Kurdish SDF forces — the most effective anti-ISIS fighters", "outcome_title": "The Kurdish Gamble", "outcome": "The SDF becomes the backbone of anti-ISIS operations, liberating Raqqa in 2017. But this infuriates Turkey — a NATO ally — who invades northern Syria. The US-NATO alliance develops a deep fracture.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Push for a political settlement including Assad's eventual departure", "outcome_title": "Geneva Goes Nowhere", "outcome": "Geneva talks continue for years. Assad, backed by Russia and Iran, has no incentive to compromise. The diplomatic track is effectively dead.", "score": -1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-002", "briefing_title": "Liberating Aleppo", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Liberating Aleppo Published: Wed, 12/14/2016 - 13:55 Updated: Tue, 08/08/2017 - 10:12 As pro-government forces recapture Aleppo from radical outfits, there is hope that some semblance of normalcy could return to the war-ravaged city. It is also an opportune moment to revisit the western narrative regarding the crisis Observed through the prism of history it was undoubtedly a blitzkrieg.  Within a space of about ten days, Aleppo’s eastern district that was under the occupation of myriad Islamic radical outfits wilted like a candle under enormous pressure mounted by the joint coalition of the Syrian Arab Army, Russian airforce and the Hezbullah militia. Success came to them in large increments: the area of occupation of Bashar Assad loyalists increased from 68 to 85 percent in one week and finally to 98 percent. It is at this point that the western media that had been romanticising the resistance of the Islamic radicals- including Abu Sakkar who posed in front of a camera eating the guts of a Syrian soldier- began to demand that the Western powers should step into the stop the 'genocide' of the 200,000 that had been trapped in the enclave that was under the militants control. The Russians who were lambasted in the UN General Assembly by US Ambassador Samantha Power for the pitilessness with which they killed children have agreed to a ceasefire and a controlled exit of the rebels to Idlib and other areas will take place soon. The victory in Aleppo is big for Syrian President Basher Al Assad, who would now control all large cities except Idlib. The city is also significant as it is the commercial capital of the country that was methodically ravaged by militants who dismantled factories, hardware and ferried the loot to neighbouring Turkey. Though half of Aleppo was always under the control of the government, the return of the entire city to the control of pro-government forces allows Assad to start thinking of the possibility of rebuilding his country once the war ends. Peace, though, is unlikely to return any time soon if the recapture of ancient city of Palmyra by ISIS is anything to go by. There are vested global and regional interests that don’t want stability in this country till their geo-strategic interests are met. The fall of Aleppo and the subsequent recapture of Palmyra are a manifestation of the serious jostling for influence that is taking place in Syria between West and the Syrian backers like Russia and Iran. What is really aggrav", "phase_num": 4, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Fall of Aleppo", "period": "December 2016", "context": "After four years of siege and months of intense Russian airstrikes, Aleppo — Syria's second city — falls to Assad's forces. The war's outcome is no longer in doubt. Assad will survive. The question is only what kind of wreckage he will rule over.", "question": "Aleppo has fallen. The war's outcome is decided. Assad will survive. What happens to Syria now?", "choices": [{"label": "Withhold reconstruction aid until a political transition is negotiated", "outcome_title": "Reconstruction as Leverage", "outcome": "Western nations refuse to fund reconstruction without political change. Assad uses Chinese and Russian investment. Reconstruction becomes a weapon — selectively rebuilding loyalist areas while leaving opposition regions in ruins.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Accept the new reality — work with Assad to manage the humanitarian catastrophe", "outcome_title": "Normalising the Abnormal", "outcome": "Pragmatism prevails. Assad is re-engaged internationally. But there is no accountability for 500,000 deaths and chemical weapons use. Assad's impunity becomes the template: brutality with Russian cover works.", "score": -2}, {"label": "Maintain the northeast Syria SDF enclave as permanent leverage", "outcome_title": "The Frozen Conflict", "outcome": "US troops remain in the Kurdish northeast — the oil-rich areas. This prevents Assad from capturing critical revenue. But it creates a permanent frozen conflict with no exit strategy.", "score": 0}]}], "outcome_title": "A Country Destroyed", "outcome_text": "Syria ended the decade with Assad in power — ruling over a broken state. 13 million displaced. 500,000 dead. The country carved into zones controlled by Assad, Kurdish forces, Turkish-backed groups, and remnant ISIS cells. Iran and Russia consolidated permanent influence. The articles in this archive — from Sanjay Kapoor's dispatches from inside Syria in 2012 to the Russia analysis — document one of the 21st century's defining geopolitical failures."}, {"id": "afpak-knot", "title": "THE AF-PAK KNOT", "subtitle": "2001 — 2021", "tagline": "Two decades, two trillion dollars, and a question no one could answer: can you win a war when your ally is also your enemy?", "color": "#a78bfa", "icon": "🏔", "phases": [{"briefing_id": "art-047", "briefing_title": "AFGHANISTAN- WHAT INDIA SHOULD EXPECT AND WHAT IT SHOULD DO", "briefing_text": "AFGHANISTAN- WHAT INDIA SHOULD EXPECT AND WHAT IT SHOULD DO Published: Mon, 03/08/2010 - 11:34 Updated: Mon, 03/08/2010 - 11:41 Vikram Sood, the former RAW chief explains the situation in Afghanistan and also puts forth the options available to India. Afghanistan has been at the cross roads of great empires and thus a scene for frequent/brutal conflict but never under the control of any outside power for long. In the last 30 years Afghanistan has seen the effects of a Communist takeover promising liberation from feudalism and assuring equality, a religious bigoted group establishing itself spearheaded by US led Islamic zeal, followed by the US attempts to give the hapless country liberty and equality. Today we once again see the return of the Taliban and the US eager to negotiate with the same ideology and the same people they wanted to overthrow in 2001. There are many who say that this is actually a display of ethnic nationalism under the guise of a religious movement. Situation in Afghanistan is a very complex one with a number of actors - internal and external, conflicting interests and capabilities. It is not likely to change in the next few years. <li>This includes the various ethnic nationalities of Afghanistan. </li>\n<li>The warlords and their vested interests in the production and smuggling of narcotics, and arms.</li>\n<li>Corruption alone is a US $ 2 billion industry. A weak government in Kabul without any viable succession option visible. </li>\n<li>Its inability to exercise any control outside Kabul is well known. It has weak army and law enforcement machineries; their growth is hindered by factors of corruption and local ethnic interests. Attempts to establish an ANA and ANP have been slow and arduous. </li>\n<li>Most importantly, there is more than one group operating inside Afghanistan and many from Pakistani soil.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are a number of external players and their own interests. The US and its allies want to make US free of any terrorism emanating from Afghanistan which is a threat to them and their allies. To do this they rely on Pakistan whose interests are different from the American interests and whose co-operation is less than forthcoming. Having made Pakistan totally indispensible to their cause, US has allowed Pakistan to play the spoiler. Pakistan, obsessed with India, has assumed that the control of Kabul slipping into the hands of the Taliban and that the Taliban being under their control would leave them in an advantage", "phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Invasion and India's Choice", "period": "October 2001", "context": "Following 9/11, the US removes the Taliban from Kabul within weeks. A new Afghan government under Hamid Karzai is installed. India faces a historic choice: how deeply to engage with the new Afghanistan while managing Pakistani opposition to any Indian presence in Kabul.", "question": "India has a historic opportunity to build deep ties with the new Afghanistan. How deep should that engagement be?", "choices": [{"label": "Full strategic engagement: invest billions, open consulates, build major infrastructure", "outcome_title": "The $3 Billion Bet", "outcome": "India commits $3 billion — roads, power lines, the Salma Dam, the Afghan Parliament. India becomes one of the most popular countries in Afghan opinion polls. But Pakistan's ISI funds Taliban attacks specifically targeting Indian personnel and projects.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Limited engagement: focus on humanitarian aid, avoid a strategic footprint", "outcome_title": "Ceding the Field", "outcome": "India avoids Pakistani ire in the short term but cedes the strategic field. Pakistan fills the space. When the Taliban returns, India has little leverage and much to lose.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Use Afghanistan as transactional leverage against Pakistan", "outcome_title": "The Pawn Problem", "outcome": "Afghans resent being used as a chip in the India-Pakistan game. India's engagement becomes transactional and shallow. The relationship never deepens — vulnerable to the first shift in the military situation.", "score": -1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-049", "briefing_title": "Af-Pak The Eagle Trapped", "briefing_text": "Af-Pak: The Eagle Trapped? Published: Wed, 02/08/2012 - 07:58 Updated: Tue, 04/03/2012 - 07:09 Let’s get the hell outta here” is the sentiment behind the exercise seeking negotiations to secure needed assurances and guarantees from sinister unholy characters </strong><br /><strong> SS Rao Sohoni Kabul Unless one electsto remain in denial, it is to be noted that in Afghanistan the decade-old war, between two sets of external powers: the US and its NATO partners on the one hand, and on the other: Pakistan, Saudi Arabia and various insurgent groups with headquarters in Pakistan, has transitioned to a stage beyond a stalemate.</p>\n<p>Till recently, the armed opposition were far from being able to overcome the US-led military coalition; and the US-led coalition was unable to suppress the insurgency.</p>\n<p>For ten years and more, the US had leaned on Pakistan for a range of facilitation; and in turn, Pakistan received from the US:&nbsp; military, political and financial aid and support.</p>\n<p>Whilst keeping up appearances of being allies, close allies to boot, the US and Pakistan operated in noteworthy respects as antagonists -- whether covertly or openly.</p>\n<p>This bizarre contradiction, basic to the Afghanistan theater, remained the bane of the war, throughout.</p>\n<p>There was chronic chagrin in NATO circles at the safe haven and support provided to insurgency from Pakistan and the refusal of the Pakistan Army to act against key insurgent concentrations in Pakistan.</p>\n<p>The Marines operation on May 2, 2011 in Abottabad, certain drone strikes, and the US helicopter gunships counterattack on the Salala Outpost on November 26, 2011, likewise infuriated the Pakistanis.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;After Abbottabad and Salala, and with Pakistan closing the supply land routes from Karachi via Torkham and Chaman to Afghanistan, the basic contradiction underlying the conflict in Afghanistan, which accounts as much for its protracted duration as for its frustrations, stands exposed in full glare.&nbsp; More than two months on, there is little sign of the supply routes interdiction easing, other than CENTCOM Chief, Gen. Mattis being scheduled to meet with Pakistan Army Chief, General Ashfaq Pervez Kayani later this month.</p>\n<p>Military and political observers are not unaware, but the western news media have thus far, understandably, been slow to report that the US-led NATO forces in Afghanistan now face disturbing supply challenges in Afghanistan.</p>\n<p>The position of US/NA", "phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Pakistan's Double Game", "period": "2007 – 2011", "context": "The Taliban insurgency is growing. US intelligence confirms elements within Pakistan's ISI are providing safe havens and strategic guidance to the Afghan Taliban and the Haqqani network. Pakistan simultaneously receives $18 billion in US aid as a 'frontline state against terrorism'.", "question": "Pakistan is demonstrably supporting the Taliban while accepting US anti-terror funds. The US is structurally trapped. What is the correct analysis?", "choices": [{"label": "Confront Pakistan publicly: cut aid, name ISI support as state terrorism", "outcome_title": "The Alliance Fractures", "outcome": "Pakistan retaliates by blocking NATO supply lines through its territory. 60% of NATO supplies go through Pakistan. The confrontation produces neither an end to ISI support nor improvement in the war.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Maintain the fiction: private pressure, public alliance, keep supply lines open", "outcome_title": "The Endless Compromise", "outcome": "The hypocrisy continues for another decade. Pakistan takes the money and manages the Taliban as a strategic asset. $2 trillion is spent. The war cannot be won because one side's 'ally' is the other side's patron.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Develop alternative supply routes via Russia, Central Asia, and Iran", "outcome_title": "The Northern Distribution Network", "outcome": "NATO's dependence on Pakistan is reduced, though not eliminated. This gives slightly more leverage to pressure Islamabad. Pakistan's central importance is reduced but not ended.", "score": 1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-051", "briefing_title": "Beating back the Taliban", "briefing_text": "Beating back the Taliban Published: Mon, 06/01/2009 - 11:03 Updated: Wed, 07/01/2015 - 10:42 Beena Sarwar \"IS THE THREAT of Talibanisation real or has it been hyped up by the media?\" asked an Australian journalist friend calling a week before the Pakistan army began its belated operation against the militants in Swat region. With no independent reporting from the area, there's only the army's word about the situation. If a rag-tag Taliban - barely 4,000 strong - are being trounced, it is hardly surprising. They face the world's fifth largest standing army. A quarter have reportedly been killed in the operation. Many are deserting, shaving off their beards and melting back into the local population. Not all are hardcore militants. Some joined the Taliban for money, were forced, or driven to avenge the casualties caused by American drone attacks. However, some still cause fear, according to reports coming from refugee camps that house an estimated 20 per cent of the over two million internally displaced persons (IDPs in development jargon) since the fighting began. The rest are living with friends, family or strangers, some of whom house up to 4,000 people on their lands. For the first time since 1971, a 'war narrative' is being developed by the media, government, army and politicians (many of whom until recently justified the Taliban's actions; during Kargil, they denied the Pakistan army's involvement). Now there are images\nof 'war hero funerals' and army 'shaheeds' (martyrs) - not all from Pakistan's dominant religion (Muslim) or ethnic group (Punjabi). Even before the army action, the wild, bearded, turbaned hordes were unlikely to take over Pakistan. This is not Afghanistan where decades of war destroyed all the systems and institutions. Nor is it Iran, where a huge urban-rural divide helped the mullahs to take over. Even conservative Pakistanis are uncomfortable with the Taliban's brand of Islam - public beheadings, corpse mutilations and floggings. There is wide adherence to Sufi values and anger at the Taliban's attacks on Sufi shrines. Pakistan has a 5,50,000 strong standing army (struggling to re-orient itself against its former allies, the jehadis, countering its historic conditioning against India), a bureaucracy geared to maintaining the status quo, and an elected Parliament. Regular interruptions to the political process have made them somewhat dysfunctional, but the only cure is to continue the process and break the pattern according to which ", "phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Surge and Its Limits", "period": "2009 – 2012", "context": "Obama announces a surge of 30,000 additional troops — and simultaneously announces their withdrawal deadline. Violence decreases in parts of Afghanistan. But the structural problems remain: corruption, Taliban safe havens in Pakistan, the withdrawal date that tells every Afghan with a choice which way to bet.", "question": "The surge has created temporary security gains but structural problems are unchanged. What endgame is possible?", "choices": [{"label": "Include the Taliban in a political settlement — negotiate a power-sharing deal in Doha", "outcome_title": "The Doha Trap", "outcome": "Talks begin in Doha. The Taliban negotiate in deliberate bad faith — using the process to gain international legitimacy while waiting out the withdrawal. When US troops leave in August 2021, the Afghan army collapses in eleven days.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Build a self-sustaining Afghan army before withdrawing — make withdrawal unconditional on timeline", "outcome_title": "The $88 Billion Army", "outcome": "Despite $88 billion spent, the army is hollowed out by corruption and structurally dependent on US air power. Without US support, it disintegrates. The institutional edifice was built on borrowed foundations.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Maintain a residual counter-terrorism force with no fixed withdrawal deadline", "outcome_title": "The Managed Stalemate", "outcome": "A smaller US presence of 5,000-8,000 troops stays. Attacks continue at a reduced level. The problem is managed but not solved. Expensive and endless — but preventing the catastrophic collapse that full withdrawal produces.", "score": 1}]}, {"briefing_id": "art-057", "briefing_title": "What’s in store for Afghanistan", "briefing_text": "Briefing text not available.", "phase_num": 4, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Taliban Return", "period": "August 2021", "context": "The Doha Agreement commits the US to full withdrawal. In August 2021, the Taliban takes complete control of Afghanistan in eleven days. The Afghan government collapses without resistance. Kabul falls. India evacuates its embassy — abandoning $3 billion of investment. Pakistan's military celebrates openly.", "question": "The Taliban control Afghanistan. India's investment is at risk. Pakistan has won its strategic objective. What is India's best response?", "choices": [{"label": "Open a low-level diplomatic mission in Kabul — protect India's economic interests pragmatically", "outcome_title": "The Realist Pivot", "outcome": "India opens a technical mission in Kabul. The Taliban reciprocate carefully — they need Indian economic ties and don't want to be entirely dependent on Pakistan. A fragile, pragmatic détente.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Use economic leverage on Pakistan — link trade normalisation to its Afghanistan policy", "outcome_title": "The Leverage Illusion", "outcome": "India conditions any improvement in India-Pakistan relations on Pakistan ending its support for the Taliban. Pakistan calls the bluff — India's leverage is limited without US backing.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Pivot to Chabahar — build Iran-routed connectivity that bypasses Pakistan entirely", "outcome_title": "The Long Game", "outcome": "Chabahar becomes India's strategic answer to CPEC. A land route through Iran to Afghanistan reduces dependence on Pakistan. Long-term thinking prevails over short-term frustration.", "score": 2}]}], "outcome_title": "The Knot That Couldn't Be Cut", "outcome_text": "The United States spent $2 trillion and twenty years in Afghanistan. The Taliban are back in power with more sophisticated weapons than they had in 2001. India's $3 billion investment is frozen. Pakistan's military has achieved its strategic goal of a friendly government in Kabul. The Af-Pak knot proved impossible to untangle because all the outside powers refused to address the core issue: Pakistan's systematic use of jihadist proxies as instruments of state policy."}, {"id": "saudi-oil", "title": "SAUDI ARABIA'S OIL WEAPON", "subtitle": "2014 — 2030", "tagline": "OPEC's price war, the Qatar blockade, Yemen's endless quagmire, and whether Vision 2030 can save the kingdom before oil becomes irrelevant", "color": "#fbbf24", "icon": "🛢", "phases": [{"phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Oil Price War", "period": "November 2014", "context": "Saudi Arabia — OPEC's swing producer — makes a fateful call: refuse to cut production despite collapsing oil prices. Brent crude falls from $115 to below $30. The strategy is designed to kill the US shale revolution, punish Iran, Russia, and Venezuela. It works on shale — temporarily. But it burns through $100 billion of Saudi reserves in one year, and forces the kingdom to issue international bonds for the first time in nearly two decades.", "question": "Saudi Arabia is sacrificing its own budget to wage an oil price war. What is the strategic logic — and will it work?", "briefing_key": "saudi 'turm-oil'", "choices": [{"label": "Kill US shale and restore OPEC's pricing power — a necessary short-term sacrifice", "outcome_title": "Shale Survives", "outcome": "US shale producers cut costs, innovate, and become dramatically more efficient at lower price points. When oil recovers to $50, shale bounces back faster than anyone anticipated. Saudi Arabia has burned $100 billion and failed its primary objective. OPEC's pricing power never fully returns.", "score": -1}, {"label": "The real target is Iran — prevent Tehran from benefiting from any nuclear deal", "outcome_title": "A Blunt Instrument", "outcome": "Iran's oil exports are already under sanctions — the price war hurts Iran far less than it hurts Saudi Arabia itself. But the message is clear: Riyadh will use oil as a weapon against any regional competitor. The strategy fails tactically but succeeds as a declaration of intent.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Saudi Arabia has miscalculated — the strategy is structurally irrational", "outcome_title": "The Fiscal Shock", "outcome": "The $100 billion burn rate forces Saudi Arabia to borrow internationally and launch Vision 2030. The price war's biggest effect is accelerating Saudi Arabia's recognition that oil dependency is existentially dangerous. MBS's transformation agenda is born from this fiscal shock.", "score": 1}], "briefing_id": "art-013", "briefing_title": "The Saudi ’turm-oil’", "briefing_text": "The Saudi ’turm-oil’ Published: Mon, 11/27/2017 - 06:41 With Saudi Arabia in the firm grip of a political crisis, India and the world must prepare for a steep rise in oil prices. The Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, synonymous with religious and social conservatism, is shaking and stirring the region. In the past several months, the Kingdom imposed a blockade on Qatar, detained Lebanese Prime Minister Saad Hariri, tightened the blockade around Yemen, declared war against Iran and arrested over two hundred persons during an anti-corruption purge, which included princes, businessmen, and other important officials. The man responsible for this coup, which will have far-reaching implications for the region, is Prince Mohammed Bin Salman (MBS), the 32-year-old heir to the Al Saud throne who in June replaced Mohammad Bin Nayef as the Crown Prince. \n\nMBS has held significant portfolios since 2015, and according to sources, has been groomed to take over since 2012. He is part of the Sudairi clan that began to rule after Ibn Saud, who had declared that his sons would continue to rule after him, died. The seven sons had been born to his favourite wife Hassa Sudairi. After his death, five sons have held power, and King Salman who is now 81, is in all likelihood the last King of the seven sons. In June, Mohammed Bin Nayef (MBN), the son of Nayef, one of King Salman’s brothers, was asked to step down in favour of Salman, who is 20 years younger than him. MBN is a counter-terror head and was head of the National Guard. The change comes after the Saudi’s attempt to organise a coup in Qatar which was stopped by the Americans. Nayef, who was close to the CIA, was unceremoniously dropped, and the allegiance council voted to install Mohammad Bin Salman as the Crown Prince. This political churning has not been seen in the history of the Kingdom since 2015 \n\n — two Crown Princes have been deposed. \n\nMohammed Bin Salman, unlike his other half-brothers, did not study abroad. He studied law at King Saud University in Riyadh and is very close to Crown Prince Zayed of the Arab Emirates. Zayed is said to be MBS’ mentor and is said to be the man making things happen behind the scene. According to reports that have surfaced, Prince Zayed — who is said to have a hand behind the Yemen blockade and the Qatar embargo — orchestrated the elevation of MBS after the US prevented a coup in Qatar against the Emir. When the US found out about the coup in Qatar, they asserted themselves through Nayef, bu"}, {"phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Qatar Blockade", "period": "June 2017", "context": "Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain, and Egypt impose a sudden air, land, and sea blockade on Qatar — a fellow GCC member. The stated grievances: Qatar's ties with Iran, its funding of the Muslim Brotherhood, and Al Jazeera's independent journalism. The underlying reality: Qatar has pursued an independent foreign policy that Saudi Arabia and the UAE find intolerable. Qatar hosts the world's largest US air base — Al Udeid — making Washington's response pivotal.", "question": "Saudi Arabia has blockaded a GCC member state. Qatar has refused all 13 demands. How does this end?", "briefing_key": "root of this crisis is qatar", "choices": [{"label": "Qatar capitulates — it cannot withstand economic pressure from four neighbours", "outcome_title": "Qatar Refuses to Fold", "outcome": "Within days of the blockade, Turkey deploys troops to Qatar. Iran opens its airspace. Qatar builds emergency food security infrastructure. The blockade fails to achieve a single of its 13 demands. Forty-one months later, it is lifted with no Qatari concessions. The Saudi-UAE calculation was wrong.", "score": -2}, {"label": "The US mediates a rapid face-saving resolution for all sides", "outcome_title": "Trump Contradicts Himself", "outcome": "Trump initially tweets support for the blockade, then reverses when his military realises its largest regional base is in the country being blockaded. US mediation is slow and ineffective. The Al-Ula declaration in 2021 formally ends the blockade but resolves nothing. The GCC's institutional coherence is exposed as illusory.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Qatar uses the crisis to permanently reduce its Gulf dependency", "outcome_title": "The Blockade Backfires", "outcome": "Qatar diversifies aggressively: deeper Turkey and Iran ties, expanded LNG exports globally, food self-sufficiency investments, successful 2022 World Cup. The blockade makes Qatar strategically more independent, not less. Saudi Arabia and the UAE discover that economic coercion against a gas-rich micro-state with a $300 billion sovereign wealth fund has limits.", "score": 1}], "briefing_id": "art-015", "briefing_title": "The root of this crisis is Qatar’s Independent Foreign Policy Expert", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy The root of this crisis is Qatar’s Independent Foreign Policy: Expert Published: Mon, 06/05/2017 - 13:39 Updated: Mon, 07/31/2017 - 09:20 Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Bahrain, the United Arab Emirates and other members of the Sunni Alliance severed ties with Qatar on June 5 on the grounds of the country covertly supporting terror organisations. This is being regarded as the largest rift in the region in years. The Gulf Arab states and Egypt have long resented Qatar’s support to the Muslim Brotherhood which they regard as a formidable terrorist organisation and a political enemy. Yemen and Eastern Libya too joined in with the diplomatic isolation. Iran has blamed Donald Trump and his recent trip to Riyadh as being a major catalyst in the severing of ties. Transport facilities to Qatar from the three countries have been shut down and their economic impact has been immediate, with Emirates, Fly Dubai and Etihad cancelling flights to and from Doha. Qatari citizens living in the Gulf countries have been given two weeks to leave the country. This blockade is expected to have serious implications due to the fact that 40 percent of the food imports into the country come from Saudi Arabia. In terms of jobs, the cancellation of transport facilities will also adversely impact the preparation for the FIFA World Cup and the construction of stadiums for the event that is to be held in the country in 2022. In an interview with Hardnews Foreign Policy, Birol Baskan, the Assistant Professor at the Georgetown University School of Foreign Service in Qatar, spoke about the causes of the diplomatic crisis and the possible outcomes of the embargo as it unfolds. What is the genesis of this extraordinary crisis?\nFour Arab states have so far cut their diplomatic relations with Qatar. Others might follow them depending on the leverage Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates have over them. Each country has possibly its own reason. Bahrain-Qatar had disputes in the 1980s over the Hawar Islands. More recently, however, Bahrain has been accusing Qatar of encouraging the Bahraini Sunnis to migrate to Qatar. One must also note that Saudi Arabia has great leverage over Bahrain. The United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Egypt are critical of Qatar’s continued support for the Muslim Brotherhood (MB), a movement that they consider aims at spreading terror. For Saudi Arabia as well, Qatar’s relationship with the Muslim Brotherhood (MB) might be an issue. It also declared the MB as a terror"}, {"phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Yemen: The Endless War", "period": "March 2015 — present", "context": "Saudi Arabia intervenes militarily in Yemen in March 2015 — expecting weeks, getting years. The Houthi forces, backed by Iran, have seized Sanaa. Eight years later: 250,000 dead, the world's worst humanitarian crisis, and Houthi missiles targeting Riyadh. The Abqaiq drone attack in 2019 — attributed to Iran via the Houthis — briefly cuts 5% of global oil supply. Saudi Arabia discovers its air force cannot win a guerrilla war.", "question": "Saudi Arabia is trapped in an unwinnable war that Iran is winning by proxy. What is the realistic exit?", "briefing_key": "saudi-yemen conflict", "choices": [{"label": "Escalate militarily — a decisive offensive will break Houthi resistance", "outcome_title": "The Unwinnable Escalation", "outcome": "The Houthis have dispersed infrastructure, adapted to airstrikes over eight years, and gained in both military capability and popular legitimacy from surviving the wealthiest Arab military. Further escalation deepens the humanitarian catastrophe and extends Iran's access to a permanent bleeding wound in Saudi Arabia's backyard.", "score": -2}, {"label": "China-brokered Saudi-Iran rapprochement opens space for Houthi negotiations", "outcome_title": "The Chinese Angle", "outcome": "The 2023 Saudi-Iran normalisation deal — brokered by Beijing, not Washington — creates the conditions for Houthi talks. Ceasefires hold for periods. The Houthis, having survived, negotiate from a position of strength. Any settlement acknowledges their permanent role in Yemeni politics — the exact outcome Saudi Arabia went to war to prevent.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Withdraw and let Yemenis determine their own future", "outcome_title": "The Unmanaged Exit", "outcome": "Saudi withdrawal without a political framework collapses into renewed civil war. The Houthis consolidate control of most of Yemen. Saudi Arabia has a hostile Iranian-linked movement on its southern border — and has spent $100 billion for the privilege of creating it.", "score": -1}], "briefing_id": "art-012", "briefing_title": "Saudi-Yemen conflict Another proxy war", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Saudi-Yemen conflict: Another proxy war? Published: Thu, 04/09/2015 - 11:05 Updated: Mon, 06/15/2015 - 11:41 Saudi airstrikes threaten to escalate the conflict in Yemen Sabika Zehra Delhi Airstrikes on the Al Mazraq refugee camp in northern Yemen on March 31 killed dozens of persons and injured hundreds. These were delivered by a Saudi-led military coalition of Gulf monarchies and other regional governments, on behalf of Yemen’s ousted president, Abdu Rabu Mansour Hadi. The Al Mazraq attack was just another instance of incessant aerial bombings being carried out in and around Sanaa and other Houthi positions as part of Operation Decisive Storm, which began on March 25. Saudi authorities maintain that the casualties were Houthi rebels – who carried out an “illegitimate coup” and took over the capital, Sanaa, six months ago, with support of former President Ali Abdullah Saleh. But reports by human rights groups have stated that most of those dead and wounded in Al Mazraq bombings were internally displaced people, living in pitiable conditions since 2009. Limited intelligence capabilities and lack of precision-guided weapons of the Saudi-led forces involved in this operation are bound to result in high collateral damage. Instability in Yemen and the external military intervention have dragged the country and the region to the brink of an abyss. The speed with which Saudi Arabia put together an anti-Houthi coalition took everyone by surprise. Even the United States stood dumbstruck as Saudi Ambassador Adel al-Jubair announced the launch of the decision of Saudi Arabia and other regional countries to intervene militarily “to defend and support the legitimate government of Yemen and prevent the radical Houthi movement from taking over the country”. The timing of the operation suggests that the Saudis meant to send a message to Washington as well as Tehran. Riyadh launched the assault, despite the massive associated risks, in its effort to draw clear red lines for the United States about changes in the regional order precipitated by the US-Iran nuclear pact. Saudi Arabia fears that its status as America’s main ally in the Arab world is imperilled by the US-Iran rapprochement. With the imminent withdrawal of embargoes and sanctions, and the presence of its own treasure trove of oil and gas resources, Tehran could challenge Riyadh’s position as a regional power. In addition to demonstrating its resolve for the benefit of the Americans, Saudi Arabia s"}, {"phase_num": 4, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Vision 2030: The Reckoning", "period": "2016 — 2030", "context": "MBS launches Vision 2030 — wean the kingdom off oil, build a tourism economy, allow women to drive, construct NEOM, diversify investments. The Khashoggi murder in 2018 tests whether the West will maintain pressure on Riyadh or accommodate the new Saudi Arabia. By 2024, oil still accounts for 60% of government revenue. NEOM remains largely a concept. But the entertainment sector has opened, women are driving, and Saudi Arabia is hosting Formula 1 races. The transformation is real — and slower than promised.", "question": "Can Saudi Arabia genuinely transform before oil becomes irrelevant — and what does the international community owe a reforming autocracy?", "briefing_key": "weaning saudi arabia", "choices": [{"label": "Vision 2030 is a genuine transformation — engage fully and accelerate it", "outcome_title": "Conditional Optimism", "outcome": "Foreign investment flows. Entertainment opens. Women enter the workforce. But the Khashoggi murder reveals that transformation is happening alongside, not instead of, brutal political repression. The economy remains oil-dependent. Each oil price spike reduces the urgency for painful structural change.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Vision 2030 is PR — the rentier state cannot reform itself", "outcome_title": "Structural Pessimism", "outcome": "The most powerful forces in Saudi society — the ulema, the tribes, the business elite — are not enthusiastic about transformation that threatens their position. Reform from the top without civil society is structurally fragile. The pattern: announce reform, do some of it, claim success, move on.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Engage with transformation but insist on accountability for Khashoggi and Yemen", "outcome_title": "The Moral Clarity Test", "outcome": "The West discovers it lacks the leverage to insist on accountability while simultaneously wanting Saudi oil, Saudi investment, and Saudi cooperation on Iran and Israel. Accountability without leverage is rhetoric. Engagement without accountability is complicity. Saudi Arabia navigates between the two, and mostly wins.", "score": 1}], "briefing_id": "art-017", "briefing_title": "Weaning Saudi Arabia off oil", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Weaning Saudi Arabia off oil Published: Fri, 05/13/2016 - 12:18 Updated: Fri, 05/13/2016 - 12:19 Prince Mohammad bin Salman's Vision 2030 document outlines a number of significant reforms that seek to change not only the Saudi economy, but state-society relations more broadly, in a way that hasn't been done since the Kingdom’s founding\nShrinivas S Sohoni Delhi The Kingdom of Saudi Arabia is the home of Islam’s holiest places attracting millions for Hajj and Umra, and, as the world’s largest producer of oil as well, is thus a pivot of the global economy and intercontinental politics. Saudi Arabia, however, stands now at a precarious juncture - in a rapidly morphing economic and political situation.  A variety of inter-connected factors have a bearing on what is happening in Saudi Arabia.  Some of these are: Oil production and pricing; Saudi-Wahhabi phobia and enmity vis-à-vis Shi’i sects, the Shi’i Islamic Republic of Iran, and President Basher Al Assad’s regime in Syria; Operations of the so-called ‘Islamic State of Iraq and Syria’ (ISIS); Saudi’s self-initiated war against Houtis in Yemen; the resultant tension and turmoil, explosive situations, Terrorism and Extremism, and conflicting Insurgencies. Following the death of King Abdullah, the ascent of King Salman bin Abdul Aziz al Saud to the Saudi throne in January 2015 brought his son, the young Prince Mohammad bin Salman, to the core of the Saud regime. Prince Mohammad bin Salman now combines in himself the roles of Deputy Crown Prince, Deputy Premier, and Defense Minister.  Acknowledged as the de facto power centre of the Saudi monarchy, he has eclipsed the designated Crown Prince and Interior Minister, Prince Mohammad bin Nayef. Known to be ambitious and bold, - considered in some quarters as rash - Prince Mohammad bin Salman launched on 25 April 2016, his ‘Vision 2030’ program – a policy paper and blueprint for growth - prepared by the eminent US consultancy, Mckinsey Global Initiative. “Vision 2030” lays out a composite program of far-reaching structural reform designed to transform Saudi Arabia from a basically oil-based economy into a dynamic variegated industrial economy not dependent chiefly on oil revenues. “Vision 2030” notes that the oil price boom from 2003 to 2013 fuelled Saudi Arabia’s prosperity.  Saudi GDP doubled, household income rose by 75 percent, and 1.7 million jobs were created. Within a decade, despite making substantial investments in Education, Health, and Infras"}], "outcome_title": "A Kingdom in Transition", "outcome_text": "Saudi Arabia exits the 2020s as the world's most important oil supplier — aware that its geopolitical leverage is eroding with every electric vehicle sold and every solar panel installed. The Yemen war is its most visible strategic failure. The Qatar crisis revealed Gulf 'unity' as a fiction. Vision 2030 is a genuine but incomplete transformation. And the Khashoggi murder revealed the character of the transformation's architect. The Hardnews archive documents a kingdom that senses its moment is passing but cannot agree — internally or externally — on what it should become next."}, {"id": "india-west-asia", "title": "INDIA'S WEST ASIA DILEMMA", "subtitle": "2005 — present", "tagline": "From the IAEA vote that tested non-alignment to Chabahar and the Gulf diaspora — how India navigates the most volatile region on earth", "color": "#facc15", "icon": "🪬", "phases": [{"phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The IAEA Vote", "period": "September 2005", "context": "India is negotiating a landmark civilian nuclear deal with the US — access to technology, fuel, and strategic partnership denied for decades. Washington makes the price clear: India must vote against Iran at the IAEA, effectively abandoning a fellow Non-Aligned Movement country under Western pressure. Prime Minister Manmohan Singh is squeezed between the Left parties who support his government and the strategic opportunity of a lifetime. It is the clearest test yet of India's 'strategic autonomy'.", "question": "India has been asked to vote against Iran at the IAEA in exchange for the US-India nuclear deal. Where does India's interest actually lie?", "briefing_key": "crossing the rubicon", "choices": [{"label": "Vote against Iran — the nuclear deal is a generational strategic opportunity", "outcome_title": "The Rubicon Crossed", "outcome": "India votes against Iran. The nuclear deal proceeds. But Iran cancels Indian participation in the South Pars gas field. The relationship with Tehran is chilled for years. India has signalled it will subordinate historic partnerships to the US relationship — a signal that has strategic consequences across the Global South.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Abstain — preserve autonomy without directly opposing the West", "outcome_title": "The Ambiguous Middle", "outcome": "India abstains. Washington is displeased but the nuclear deal survives. Tehran is less angry. India preserves the form of its independence while signalling it won't take decisive positions on either side. A characteristically Indian outcome — strategic ambiguity that pleases no one but injures no one either.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Vote with Iran — non-alignment and the Global South relationship must come first", "outcome_title": "The Deal Collapses", "outcome": "The nuclear deal with the US collapses. India loses access to civilian nuclear technology that it has been denied since 1974. The strategic partnership with America is set back a decade. India's independence is preserved — but the cost in strategic capability and economic opportunity is enormous.", "score": -1}], "briefing_id": "art-082", "briefing_title": "Crossing the Rubicon", "briefing_text": "Crossing the Rubicon Submitted by Hardnews on Wed, 11/02/2005 - 12:05 India’s foreign policy is passing through interesting times. The strategic partnership with the United States of America is a paradigmatic shift from a non-aligned worldview to one dealing with the imperatives of a unipolar world. But, can we trust the United States? Sanjay Kapoor Delhi In 1952, Mahatma Gandhi's personal emissary and director of India's first community development project (CDP), Sudhir Ghosh, hit upon a novel idea, which he thought would change the face of the newly-independent India. He proposed to use refugees from Pakistan to build towns. His argument was that if he could be loaned the funds that the government would be using for looking after 40,000 refugees for the next three years, then he could create employment for them and out of their labour build a town that would provide permanent means of livelihood. India's first prime minister Jawaharlal Nehru jumped on the idea and extended all help. The town of Faridabad, near Delhi, was the outcome of this enterprise. But this is just part of the story as Ghosh reveals in his book, Gandhi's Emissary. Building a town from the efforts of a community of refugees was an experiment that had not been attempted in the post-colonial world and held out interesting possibilities for future development of such societies. There was the understandable hype about Faridabad and what Ghosh had achieved. International recognition too followed. But Nehru suddenly chose to withdraw himself from the project. Ghosh explains in his book, \"The withdrawal of Nehru's mantle came, strangely enough, through the interest that my American friends showed in the Faridabad experiment\". Nehru's ire at anyone cavorting with the US had a reason. Immediately after independence, Indian leadership expected support from US government, which was an anti-colonial power and a democracy, but it found them indifferent. During Pandit Nehru's visit to Washington, it became clear to him that the US wanted to use India as a lever against China. Nehru was keen to elicit US support as both China and Soviet Union were opposed to India on the issue of Kashmir. But that help was not forthcoming.He made it clear that India needed help, but not at any cost. \"They expected more than gratitude and goodwill and that more I could not supply,\" said a disappointed Nehru. Between 1947 and 1949, the US ambassador to India, Henry Grady, told the Indian leadership that US would not i"}, {"phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Iran Pipeline", "period": "2009 — 2014", "context": "The Iran-Pakistan-India gas pipeline — called the 'peace pipeline' — would bring Iranian gas through Pakistan to energy-hungry India at a fraction of the cost of LNG imports. It is potentially transformative: solving India's energy deficit, deepening India-Iran ties, and perhaps building an India-Pakistan economic relationship around shared infrastructure. The US is firmly opposed, threatening sanctions. Pakistan wants escalating transit fees. Iran wants a long-term anchor customer. India is paralysed.", "question": "The IPI pipeline could solve India's energy problem and build regional connectivity — but at the cost of the US relationship. Does India build it?", "briefing_key": "india can be one of the first", "choices": [{"label": "Build the pipeline — India's energy security must come before US preferences", "outcome_title": "The Sanctions Trap", "outcome": "India joins the IPI. US threatens sanctions precisely as the civilian nuclear deal is being implemented. New sanctions hit Iran before the pipeline is complete. India has paid the diplomatic cost of defying Washington and received no gas. The project stalls indefinitely.", "score": -1}, {"label": "Drop IPI under US pressure — pursue LNG and deepen the strategic partnership", "outcome_title": "The Energy Gap", "outcome": "India drops IPI. The US strategic partnership deepens. But India remains dependent on expensive LNG imports from the Gulf. The energy security problem is deferred, not solved. Iran is disappointed. When the JCPOA creates an opening for Iran's re-engagement, India has already ceded the ground.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Pivot to Chabahar — build connectivity without the pipeline and without Pakistani transit", "outcome_title": "The Strategic Pivot", "outcome": "India pivots to Chabahar port — a land route to Afghanistan and Central Asia that bypasses Pakistan entirely. This satisfies connectivity goals while avoiding the most direct confrontation with US sanctions. India preserves strategic flexibility and builds a new vector of influence in the region.", "score": 2}], "briefing_id": "art-098", "briefing_title": "‘India can be one of the first countries to benefit from the Iran nuclear deal_", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy ‘India can be one of the first countries to benefit from the Iran nuclear deal' Published: Mon, 08/10/2015 - 11:05 Updated: Wed, 09/16/2015 - 10:56 The Iranian Ambassador to India, Gholamreza Ansari, does not really pull punches when it comes to talking about the role played by Israel in its endeavour to scuttle the nuclear deal. In a freewheeling interview to Hardnews, he says that Israel is desperate to gain acceptance with India and other countries. He does not think that the Islamic State is sectarian as it has killed more Sunnis, whom it claims to represent, than Shias. He is intrigued by the fact that the Islamic State, which seems so dangerous, has never threatened Israel. He feels India could be the first country to benefit once the embargo is lifted from Iran. Excerpts: Sanjay Kapoor & Sadiq Naqvi Delhi How do you perceive the changing equation between the Israelis and the Americans following the Iran nuclear deal? The hostility which is escalating between the Americans and the Israelis is not very simple. It seems that the Americans are feeling that Israel is becoming very costly for them, although they feel that they must support Israel. Maybe there are no doubts in many politicians’ minds in America that they must support Israel, but there are a number of people who are feeling that the support of Israel is becoming very costly, and maybe counterproductive, and this is why Israel is trying to create new political links and revealing their old connections. For example, one is India. They are trying to reveal, ‘Okay, we used to have a very long history of relations, we were very good, and we were very cooperative,’ and so on. It is a show of relations. Same with the Saudis. The Israelis are trying to bring their relations with the Saudis to the forefront now. The way they are inviting people, like they invited Muslims to a seminar or talk in Lucknow and in Delhi. What seminar/talk took place in Delhi? It wasn’t a very well-known institution. The Muslims who participated said it was organised by the Israelis and the Saudis. You know, they are trying to normalise, show some sort of normalisation of Israel’s relations with many countries. They want to say that they are becoming an acceptable country to the public. They are showing ‘how the Saudis are trying to reach us’, ‘how we used to have very long relations with the Indians’, and so on. So it seems that the Israelis are in a very different situation. So they are doing it out of de"}, {"phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 4, "title": "Chabahar — India's Long Game", "period": "2016 — present", "context": "India signs the Chabahar port agreement with Iran in 2016 — a $500 million investment in Iran's southeastern port, giving India direct access to Afghanistan and Central Asia, bypassing Pakistan. It is India's strategic answer to China's CPEC. But Trump's JCPOA withdrawal, US sanctions, slow implementation, and internal bureaucratic hesitation have plagued the project. In 2024, Iran signs a separate deal with China for Chabahar — raising questions about India's seriousness.", "question": "Chabahar is India's most strategically important regional infrastructure project — but it is hostage to US sanctions and India's own hesitation. How does India handle this?", "briefing_key": "chabahar key to sidestepping", "choices": [{"label": "Push forward regardless — invoke humanitarian exemptions, argue for a permanent carve-out", "outcome_title": "The Exemption Gambit", "outcome": "India invokes exemptions for Afghan trade and presses Washington for a permanent Chabahar carve-out. The US grants limited exemptions under pressure. Phase one is completed. India maintains its foothold. But full implementation remains hostage to each US election cycle's Iran policy — a structural vulnerability.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Link Chabahar to a broader India-Iran framework — oil, investment, connectivity together", "outcome_title": "The Comprehensive Framework", "outcome": "India uses Chabahar as an anchor for comprehensive India-Iran normalisation — oil purchases, investment flows, diplomatic reset. Iran wants this. But US sanctions constrain India's ability to pay Iran even for port construction. The framework is aspirational; the implementation is fragmented.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Accept that Chabahar is long-term — don't overinvest against a sanctions-constrained partner", "outcome_title": "China Fills the Gap", "outcome": "India hedges its Chabahar commitment. China signs its own Chabahar development deal with Iran in 2024. India's connectivity ambitions in Central Asia remain dependent on either Pakistani goodwill or Chinese-built infrastructure. A strategic setback that could have been avoided with more decisive earlier commitment.", "score": -1}], "briefing_id": "art-090", "briefing_title": "Chabahar Key to sidestepping Pakistan", "briefing_text": "Chabahar: Key to sidestepping Pakistan Published: Fri, 12/02/2016 - 12:41 Updated: Tue, 08/08/2017 - 10:16 India needs to get its act together on Iran’s Chabahar Port Iran wants India to hasten the construction of the three jetties that have been allotted to it in Chabahar so that the agreement acquires meaning. Incidentally, India’s speed of project execution does not square with Iran’s expectations. Iranian foreign minister Javad Zarif, who will be in Amritsar on December 4 to attend the sixth edition of Heart of Asia conference, organised to explore regional solutions to stabilising war-torn Afghanistan, is expected to present Chabahar and the trilateral agreement as an option to shore up the damaged Afghan economy. India-Iran-Afghanistan had signed this transit agreement earlier in May this year. Chabahar provides a strategic and commercial option to India to sidestep Pakistan, which has blocked India’s land access to Afghanistan and Central Asia. An all-weather port in Iran’s Baloch-Sistan province, Chabahar is barely 60 km from Pakistan’s upcoming China-funded Gwadar Port. India’s investment here gained urgency after significant Chinese investment — $46 billion — in the 2,400-km China-Pakistan Economic Corridor (CPEC). This corridor permanently blocks India from taking the land route to reach Afghanistan or beyond. Though still very sleepy, Chabahar’s strategic location and the contestation that could take place between countries with competing expectations could turn it into a port of intrigue. Pakistanis and Chinese are always suspicious of Indian intentions in Balochistan, and now Gwadar. Not too long ago, Pakistan had arrested an alleged RAW agent, Kulbhushan Jadhav, who operated out of Chabahar as a businessman. About 900 km from the Adani-owned Mundra Port in Gujarat, this part of Iran has not seen any reporting in many years. Journalists were not encouraged in the past, but Iran is changing now. This writer is the first beneficiary of this new Iranian approach. There are just two flights to Chabahar from Tehran. The aircraft of Iran Air lands in this Second World War desert strip located in the middle of nowhere — a town interestingly called Konarak. A newer airport is coming up near the port’s free zone. It’s a magnificent road from Konarak Airport that circuits the grand muddy hills with Martian features and the fishing village of Tees to the emerging port city. A few fancy five-star hotels have come up in the last few years that suggest tha"}, {"phase_num": 4, "phase_total": 4, "title": "The Gulf Diaspora", "period": "Ongoing", "context": "8.9 million Indians live and work in the Gulf — sending home $40 billion a year in remittances, more than India's defence budget. They are India's most important demographic presence in West Asia. Yet Indian foreign policy historically underweighted Gulf relations in favour of Iran or the US. Modi's personal diplomacy in Riyadh, Abu Dhabi, and Doha partly redressed this. But Gulf states also fund Pakistani infrastructure and Wahhabi institutions across India's own heartland. The relationship is simultaneously India's most economically important and most politically underexamined.", "question": "India has 8.9 million people in the Gulf whose welfare depends on these relationships. What should India actually want from the Gulf — beyond remittances?", "briefing_key": "we are confused", "choices": [{"label": "Keep it transactional — protect diaspora, secure oil supply, maintain stability", "outcome_title": "The Comfortable Minimum", "outcome": "India maintains the Gulf relationship as a managed transaction. Gulf states continue investing in Pakistani infrastructure and funding religious institutions across India. India has no strategic leverage over Gulf policies that directly affect its domestic security environment. Safe but insufficient.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Raise the Gulf-Pakistan-extremism nexus directly — use diaspora leverage", "outcome_title": "The Quiet Conversation", "outcome": "India privately raises Gulf funding of Pakistani extremism in bilateral meetings. Post-Khashoggi, the UAE and Saudi Arabia are more receptive to Indian concerns. Progress is slow and unverifiable. But a new precedent is set: India can discuss internal security concerns in the context of the economic relationship.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Build genuine strategic partnerships — defence, investment, the India-ME-Europe corridor", "outcome_title": "The Elevation", "outcome": "Modi's Gulf diplomacy deepens into genuine strategic partnerships. UAE becomes India's third-largest trading partner. Saudi Arabia joins the India-Middle East-Europe Corridor. The Gulf relationship is elevated from diaspora management to active geopolitics — India as a structural partner in Gulf stability, not merely a labour-exporting country.", "score": 2}], "briefing_id": "art-084", "briefing_title": "‘We are confused! The onus lies on India to walk the talk’", "briefing_text": "‘We are confused! The onus lies on India to walk the talk’ Published: Mon, 08/03/2015 - 09:59 Updated: Wed, 09/16/2015 - 10:56 Palestinian Ambassador to New Delhi, Adnan Abu Alhaija was recently in news for expressing his disappointment over Indian decision to abtain from UN Human rights commission vote against Israel. Speaking to Hardnews, Ambassador Alhaija said that his country was confused towards India. However, he said that it was not bothered about the India’s bilateral ties with Israel as long as it followed the traditional “line  of Mahatma Gandhi and Nehru.” On Islamic State, Ambassador said that it has caused harm to India also, as many of its people are under its captivity (Palestinian authorities are trying to help in their release).  Ambassador also praised the Iran deal and hoped that the US will show similar perseverance to sort out the Palestinian problem. Excerpts Sanjay Kapoor and Sadiq Naqvi Delhi How do you look at India’s decision to abstain from the crucial voting on the United Nations Human Rights Council (UNHRC) resolution? It was an unfortunate decision and caught us by surprise. We have always been promised unwavering political support by the Indian government and this latest decision was not in line with the promises made. We expressed our concerns, and our fear was assuaged by the Indian government. We were told that it should not be seen as a departure from India’s traditional stand. We accepted the reasoning.  However, just a couple of days after this assurance, India again abstained from a crucial vote in the United Nations (UN) on Palestine. The UN’s Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC) approved the accreditation of the London-based Palestine Return Centre. Israel put up a resolution opposing the accreditation and India again abstained from voting. Sweden and Portugal voted against Israel’s objection. Clearly, we are very confused now. The onus lies on India to walk the talk. Do you think the government of Narendra Modi is recalibrating India’s relations with the Israelis? Modi will be the first Indian Prime Minister to visit Israel… What India does with Israel on a bilateral level is none of our concern. Our concern only starts when the issue of Palestine is involved. India has all the right to maintain whatever relation it wants with Israel. However, on the issue of the creation of an independent and sovereign state of Palestine, we do expect India to follow the traditional line it has followed since the days of Mahatma G"}], "outcome_title": "An Emerging Power, Still Uncertain", "outcome_text": "India's West Asia policy over two decades reflects a country that has grown in strategic ambition but not always in strategic clarity. The IAEA vote — to this day debated — marked India's tilt toward the West. The Iran pipeline saga revealed the cost of that tilt in energy security. Chabahar remains India's most important unrealised strategic project, now partially encircled by Chinese investments. And the Gulf diaspora has finally pushed Indian foreign policy into active Gulf engagement. The Hardnews archive — particularly Sanjay Kapoor's long-running analysis of India's confusion and clarity in equal measure — documents an emerging power still working out what it actually wants from the most volatile neighbourhood on earth."}, {"id": "arab-spring", "title": "THE ARAB SPRING UNRAVELS", "subtitle": "2010 — 2016", "tagline": "A generation demanded democracy. Mubarak fell. Gaddafi died. Assad survived. What went wrong — and why did it matter?", "color": "#34d399", "icon": "🌿", "phases": [{"phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 3, "title": "Egypt: Democracy's False Dawn", "period": "2011 – 2013", "context": "Eighteen days in Tahrir Square end Hosni Mubarak's 30-year rule. Mohamed Morsi of the Muslim Brotherhood wins Egypt's first free election. For the first time in its modern history, Egypt has a democratically elected civilian government. Washington is nervous. The Egyptian military is strategically positioned. Saudi Arabia and the UAE are writing cheques. Within a year, Morsi has concentrated power, alienated secular Egyptians, mismanaged the economy, and walked into the military's trap.", "question": "The Arab world's most populous country has elected an Islamist government in a free vote. How should the international community respond to this moment?", "briefing_key": "arab spring's partisan media", "choices": [{"label": "Fully engage with Morsi — democracy means accepting results you don't like", "outcome_title": "Engagement Without Conditions", "outcome": "The West engages with Morsi. But Morsi governs badly — concentrating power through a constitutional decree, alienating secular Egyptians, failing the economy. Massive protests erupt. The military uses this as cover for its coup. Democracy has been attempted — and failed. The question is whether it was ever given real conditions to succeed.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Support the military as the stabilising force — Egypt is too important to risk", "outcome_title": "Sisi's Egypt", "outcome": "General Sisi takes power in July 2013. The US continues $1.3 billion in military aid and declines to call it a coup. 800 protesters are killed in the Rabaa massacre. Egypt returns to authoritarian rule more complete than Mubarak's. The message to the Arab world: democratic elections are acceptable; democratic governance by Islamists is not.", "score": -2}, {"label": "Make US aid conditional on the military respecting the electoral outcome", "outcome_title": "Leverage Without Will", "outcome": "Conditionality is threatened but never enforced. The US needs Egypt's cooperation on Gaza, Sinai, and the Suez Canal. Egypt is too strategically important to sanction. The leverage is exposed as illusory — and Sisi reads this correctly. Democratic conditionality without strategic will is just rhetoric.", "score": -1}], "briefing_id": "art-087", "briefing_title": "Reasserting Islamic Identity", "briefing_text": "Reasserting Islamic Identity Published: Wed, 10/03/2012 - 08:06 Updated: Fri, 10/12/2012 - 10:16 Salman Rushdie, who has been on the run ever since the Iranian religious leader, Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini, passed a fatwa against him for defaming the Prophet, Muhammad, in his book, The Satanic Verses, has been reflecting on the growing radicalisation of Islam. He reminisces about how, when he was growing up, the world’s great Muslim cities were all very cosmopolitan. He is correct in this observation, which many of those who are baying for his blood would readily share. While Rushdie’s experience of Islamic intolerance has a lot to do with his own conduct and the manner in which he put his hand in the beehive and demanded from the bees that they do not sting him, anyone can bear testimony to the fact that Muslim communities have become extremely radicalised ever since ideology collapsed with the fall of the Berlin Wall. Anyone who lived through the 1970s and ’80s in preponderantly Muslim cities like Karachi and Lahore would testify they were more progressive and modern than any city in India. My close friend in Lucknow, Zafar Rizvi, who often visited Karachi, where he had relatives, would give the impression of having returned from Europe. He would talk about Karachi’s wealthy society, its culture and emancipated women. “They smoke and drink and wear sexy clothes,” he would intone to our adolescent imaginations. In comparison, Lucknow’s famed high life was confined to sepia tinted images in old albums. The scars of Partition, so visible in other cities of northern India, were non-existent in this deeply secular city of nawabs. Festivals such as Holi, Diwali and Eid were occasions to witness secularism as Hindus and Muslims stepped out to embrace one another and partake in the other community’s cheer. Perhaps it had something to do with Lucknow’s former Shia rule or the fact that both communities fought together against the British during the1857 revolt. Other cities of north India, in contrast, did not evidence similar bonhomie between the communities. The grand idea of “socialist and secular government” to heal the wounds of Partition might not have taken off due to the compulsions of electoral politics and the manner in which political parties resorted to riots to consolidate their support bases, yet there was communal harmony in the big cities well into the late 1980s. In the great cities of the Muslim world -— Baghdad, Damascus, Basra, Kabul, which wer"}, {"phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 3, "title": "Libya: The Intervention's Aftermath", "period": "2011 – 2015", "context": "NATO's 'responsibility to protect' intervention removes Gaddafi within months. It is the Arab Spring's only successful foreign military intervention — initially. Then Libya fragments into warring militias, two competing governments, and a weapons-flood across the Sahel that destabilises Mali, Niger, and fuels the Syrian arms market. The migrant crisis through Libya becomes Europe's most politically destabilising issue of the decade. The lesson of Libya directly shapes Russia and China's absolute refusal to allow any UN action on Syria.", "question": "Libya's intervention was a military success and a political catastrophe. What does it reveal about 'humanitarian intervention' as a doctrine?", "briefing_key": "the escalation before war", "choices": [{"label": "The intervention was necessary — Gaddafi would have massacred Benghazi", "outcome_title": "A Necessary But Incomplete Intervention", "outcome": "Perhaps. But 'stopping a massacre' is not the same as 'building a state'. NATO had no plan for the day after Gaddafi. The absence of a post-conflict political framework made fragmentation inevitable. The intervention saved lives in Benghazi — and created conditions that cost thousands more lives across the Sahel and in the Mediterranean.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Russia and China were correct to warn against regime change exceeding the mandate", "outcome_title": "The Veto Legacy", "outcome": "Their legal argument was valid — 'protecting civilians' became 'regime change'. But their alternative was allowing Gaddafi to crush an uprising. Libya's failure hardened Russia and China into absolute vetoes of any UN action on Syria. Libya's precedent directly caused Syria's far worse outcome. A warning against intervention that enabled a greater catastrophe.", "score": -1}, {"label": "The lesson is fundamental: never intervene without a political plan", "outcome_title": "The Doctrine Problem", "outcome": "The correct lesson. Military power can create space — it cannot fill it. Every successful post-conflict scenario requires a political framework before or alongside the military operation. Libya had none. The international community intervened with military capability and political vacuum in equal measure.", "score": 1}], "briefing_id": "art-088", "briefing_title": "THE ESCALATION BEFORE WAR", "briefing_text": "THE ESCALATION BEFORE WAR Published: Thu, 07/17/2014 - 07:29 Updated: Wed, 08/13/2014 - 11:46 By a rough estimate, more than 32,000 books and treatises have been written on the First World War—or the Great War, as it is known—since it erupted 100 years ago. This was a brutal conflict, in which more than 10 million people died in Europe, Africa and Asia. Empires were obliterated, societies crushed and borders redrawn. Nothing was left in the war-ravaged area after the hostilities ended. Nearly all books about this war address the central question of why it was ever fought. No one really has a real clue. Was it just the murder of Austria’s Archduke Franz Ferdinand and his wife by a Serbian nationalist in Sarajevo on June 28, 1914? Or did it have to do with something as bizarre as the compelling logic of military mobilisation, which means that once the top brass take a decision to move the troops and go to war it becomes difficult for them to back off? The latter, at least, is the way the European logic of war unpacked itself. Besides the usual centenaries that are observed for such events, interest in the First World War has galloped because of the simple fact that large areas whose borders were settled then are in the throes of unending violence. The question that is repeatedly popping up in academic articles and in columns is: Can the sectarian battles that are being fought in West Asia and Africa result in something bigger?—like a full blown World War? Even without being declared, there is a common theme that spans the violent turbulence that has besieged countries like Egypt, Syria, Libya, Iraq, Yemen, Lebanon, Bahrain, Afghanistan, and Pakistan. All these countries were\neither destabilised by West-inspired invasion or were collateral casualties of it. The First World War struck a death blow to the Ottoman Empire, believed then to be the Sunni Caliphate of the World. Borders were redrawn by British and French colonialists who were unmindful of social composition and history. Turkey lost status and land. Cities like Iraq’s Mosul—recently overrun by a motley group of Muslim warriors under the rubric of the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIL) seeking to erase the arbitrary borders established by the Sykes-Picot agreement—have long been eyed by the Turks. The rise of socialism after the October Revolution in 1917 in Russia may have de-legitimised the influence of religion in matters of the state, but it was not long before religion staged a\nviolent c"}, {"phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 3, "title": "What the Arab Spring Revealed", "period": "2011 – 2016", "context": "Five years on: Tunisia holds a fragile democracy. Egypt is back under military rule, more complete than Mubarak's. Libya is a failed state with two governments and multiple militias. Syria is a catastrophe. Bahrain's uprising was crushed with Saudi tanks. Yemen is at war. The monarchies — Jordan, Morocco, the Gulf states — have survived. The republics have mostly failed. The Islamist parties elected in free votes have been removed or discredited. A generation's hopes have been extinguished.", "question": "The Arab Spring has largely failed. What is the most honest assessment of why?", "briefing_key": "middle-east it didn't rain", "choices": [{"label": "External interference — Gulf states, Western ambivalence, Iranian manoeuvring — sabotaged it", "outcome_title": "External Sabotage, Partial Truth", "outcome": "Gulf money flowed to Islamist factions. The US was ambivalent about Islamist democratic outcomes. Iran exploited every vacuum. But this explanation lets Arab political culture and institutions off the hook entirely. External interference could not have produced such comprehensive failure if domestic conditions for democratic consolidation had existed.", "score": 0}, {"label": "The structural conditions for democracy — civil institutions, rule of law, free press — did not exist", "outcome_title": "Institutional Deficit", "outcome": "Democratic governance requires functioning civil institutions: an independent judiciary, free press, rule of law, civil society. None of these existed after decades of authoritarian rule that deliberately destroyed them. Elections are not democracy. The Arab Spring called elections before building the foundations that make them meaningful.", "score": 1}, {"label": "The region's powers — Saudi Arabia above all — were committed to its failure", "outcome_title": "The Counter-Revolution", "outcome": "Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and other Gulf monarchies spent billions to ensure the Arab Spring failed. They saw democratic Islamist governance as an existential threat to monarchical legitimacy. The counter-revolution was systematic, well-funded, and largely successful. Democratic movements were not just failing — they were being actively terminated.", "score": 1}], "briefing_id": "art-040", "briefing_title": "Middle-East It didn’t rain on its own", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Middle-East: It didn’t rain on its own Published: Wed, 05/27/2015 - 07:22 Updated: Tue, 06/16/2015 - 08:21 The Arab Spring that saw regime changes in Egypt, Libya and Yemen, and unending violence in Syria was choreographed elsewhere Sanjay Kapoor Delhi In early 2008, I was in Cairo a few days after the food riots broke out in Mahalla-el-Kubra, the textile city\nof Egypt. Cairo is a noisy and disorderly city, where chaos stands out as defiance against the authoritarian leaders who have ruled Egypt for many years. The streets were crawling with edgy security personnel in black dungarees and carrying Kalashnikovs. Hotels, too, were on heightened alert. Fierce dogs sniffed hotel guests before they could enter the lobby. We were told law and order had collapsed in the last few days and tourists were discouraged to go to the pyramids of Giza unless their vehicles were escorted by armoured carriers. Egypt’s government under President Hosni Mubarak was losing credibility, but was conscious of history. He did not want a repetition of 1952 when the government of the day was ousted during food riots. Despite the turmoil and the raging protests that were also known as “IMF riots” in different parts of Egypt, no one really thought they were enough to bring down the government. In some ways, these riots showed the erosion of the control communities exercised on the economy and especially over the food market. When governments are compelled to adopt IMF policies and slash subsidies and make food expensive, people get angry everywhere. As I wrote in Hardnews in April 2008, the government machinery went into overdrive and doubled the wages of the striking textile workers. Cleverly, they tried to show the incident as an aberration rather than a manifestation of a deeply infected body politic. In a matter of a few days, the happenings in Mahalla were dismissed as the typical “food riots” that hit parts of Egypt in the early part of the year. [See more at: http://bit.ly/1H8pFba] What was visible in Cairo then was the collapse of the moral economy, which had nothing to do with the one praised by multilateral bodies like the IMF and World Bank. Ordinary people experienced what Amartya Sen would describe as a “failure of entitlement” and they stepped out to demand their rights. The Middle East-North Africa area was the fastest growing region of the world at the time. Despite being hit by the global slowdown in 2008, it still managed to grow at a steady pace of 3 pe"}], "outcome_title": "The Long Winter", "outcome_text": "The Arab Spring is the defining political event of the 21st century's second decade — and the defining failure of the aspiration that social media and people power could overcome entrenched authoritarian systems without the institutional foundations to replace them. Tunisia — the only country where it partially worked — has since reverted to one-man rule under Saied. The broader lesson, documented across the Hardnews archive, is that political transformation requires not just the will to change — which was abundantly present in Tahrir and Deraa and Misrata — but the institutions, regional support, and international goodwill to consolidate change. None of these were present in sufficient measure. The Spring became a long winter."}, {"id": "iran-shadow", "title": "IRAN'S SHADOW EMPIRE", "subtitle": "2006 — present", "tagline": "How Iran built a network of proxy forces from Beirut to Baghdad to Sanaa — and what it costs Tehran to maintain the 'Axis of Resistance'", "color": "#c084fc", "icon": "🕸", "phases": [{"phase_num": 1, "phase_total": 3, "title": "Hezbollah and the Lebanon Equation", "period": "2006 – 2016", "context": "The 2006 Israel-Hezbollah war reshapes the region. Hezbollah — created, trained, and funded by Iran's Revolutionary Guards after 1982 — fights the Israeli military to a standstill for 34 days. It emerges from the war politically stronger despite military damage. Iran has demonstrated that a non-state proxy force, equipped with sophisticated missiles and disciplined fighters, can deter the Middle East's most powerful military. Hezbollah becomes Iran's most valuable strategic asset — and a model for every subsequent proxy.", "question": "Hezbollah has demonstrated that Iranian proxy warfare can deter a conventional military. What does this mean for regional security?", "briefing_key": "geopolitical jigsaw", "choices": [{"label": "This proves Iran's strategy works — proxy forces give it deniable military reach far beyond its borders", "outcome_title": "The Proxy Template", "outcome": "The Hezbollah model becomes Iran's template across the region: the Houthis in Yemen, Popular Mobilisation Forces in Iraq, various factions in Syria. Each proxy gives Iran deniable power projection and a 'forward defence' posture. The strategy is cheaper than conventional military and politically survivable in ways direct action is not.", "score": 0}, {"label": "This will provoke a devastating Israeli/US counter-response that destroys both Hezbollah and Iran's programme", "outcome_title": "The Deterrence Calculus", "outcome": "Despite multiple provocations, neither Israel nor the US have launched the comprehensive counter-response that would decisively degrade Hezbollah. The mutual deterrence holds — both sides understand that a full war would be devastatingly costly. Hezbollah's missile arsenal grows to over 130,000. The threat is managed, not eliminated.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Iran is building a strategic liability — maintaining proxies costs more than the benefits", "outcome_title": "The Long-Term Drain", "outcome": "Hezbollah costs Iran an estimated $700 million a year. The Popular Mobilisation Forces in Iraq require billions more. Syria's survival cost Iran hundreds of lives and tens of billions. The 'Axis of Resistance' is also an axis of economic burden — one that grows as sanctions tighten Iran's fiscal space.", "score": 1}], "briefing_id": "art-096", "briefing_title": "Iran A geopolitical jigsaw puzzle", "briefing_text": "Iran: A geopolitical jigsaw puzzle Published: Wed, 07/13/2016 - 12:30 Updated: Wed, 07/13/2016 - 12:31 In an interview with Hardnews, Dr Sujata Ashwarya, a prominent academic and noted expert on the Middle East, talks about the various implications of Iran’s rehabilitation in the international pecking order, the impact of the US presidential elections on Iran and the long-term consequences of the Chabahar deal between India and Iran Sanjay Kapoor Delhi How do you see the return of Iran in the international order after the signing of the P5+1 deal? Will it aggravate contestation with Saudi Arabia and Israel? The nuclear deal has reinforced Iran’s capacity to pursue its regional ambitions more aggressively, and even aspire for a global role. Iran’s regional influence has been growing steadily since the Iraq War in 2003. Saddam Hussein’s downfall removed the proverbial ‘gatekeeper of the Arab east against the revolutionary regime in Persia’, to the great trepidation of Saudi Arabia and other Arab Gulf countries. Shia ascendance in Iraq shaped the arc of Iranian influence from Baghdad to Beirut. Iran is assisting Baghdad in taking on the Islamic State (IS), and along with its proxy Hizbullah, Tehran is helping President Assad fight the rebels and the IS in Syria – via logistic support and training as well as supply of weapons. The nuclear deal has also empowered Iran to stretch its sphere of influence and change the situation in Yemen by making the war costly, both in material and human casualties, for Saudi Arabia and its allies. Iran’s assistance to the Houthis (who belong to the Zaydi sect, also known as Fivers, of Islam) in Yemen, right on the Saudi doorstep, brings the Iranian arc of influence full circle, both literally and figuratively. Therefore, normalisation of Iran has come in the wake of unprecedented Iranian influence in the entire region, much to the chagrin of both Saudi Arabia and Israel, who have opposed the nuclear deal. The Saudis are apprehensive of the strategic leverage and latitude it would give to Iranian pursuits, when the tag of a ‘pariah state’ is gone and the dividend from sanctions relief takes effect. Israel, or in any case, the current dispensation there, is convinced that Iran will rescind and cheat somewhere along the line, diluting the Jewish state’s regional military supremacy. In view of Arab failings, starkly evident in the events of the Arab Spring, the P5+1 deal is also indicative of America’s recognition of Iran as a pla"}, {"phase_num": 2, "phase_total": 3, "title": "Syria: Iran's Most Expensive Proxy War", "period": "2011 – 2019", "context": "When Assad faces collapse in 2013, Iran makes a decisive choice: preserve its land bridge to Hezbollah at almost any cost. Iran deploys advisors, then fighters, then the full weight of Hezbollah and Iraqi Shia militias. The investment is staggering — estimates suggest $30-50 billion and hundreds of Iranian deaths, including several senior IRGC commanders. But by 2016, with Russian air power complementing Iranian ground forces, Assad survives. Iran has secured its most important strategic investment: a land corridor from Tehran through Baghdad and Damascus to Beirut.", "question": "Iran has saved Assad — at enormous cost. What has Iran actually won, and is the Syria investment strategically sound?", "briefing_key": "liberating aleppo", "choices": [{"label": "Iran has won decisively — the land corridor to Hezbollah is priceless", "outcome_title": "The Strategic Prize", "outcome": "The Tehran-Baghdad-Damascus-Beirut land bridge gives Iran permanent logistical access to its most important proxy. Weapons, fighters, and equipment can move overland, largely beyond Israeli air interdiction. It is Iran's single most important strategic accomplishment in the region — and it required Syria's survival to achieve.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Iran has won the battle but is bleeding from the cost — Syria is a strategic overstretch", "outcome_title": "The Pyrrhic Victory", "outcome": "Preserving Assad costs Iran billions annually in reconstruction support and ongoing military presence. Syria becomes a recurring drain on Iranian resources precisely as sanctions tighten the domestic economy. The victory creates obligations — not just assets. Iran is now responsible for propping up a state that cannot stand without it.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Israel's sustained strikes on Iranian positions in Syria will eventually make the corridor untenable", "outcome_title": "The Continuous Attrition", "outcome": "Israel has conducted over 350 strikes on Iranian and Hezbollah positions in Syria since 2013. The strikes degrade but do not eliminate Iran's presence. Iran rebuilds, adapts, disperses. The corridor is degraded but not severed. A low-intensity war of attrition that neither side can decisively win — or safely stop.", "score": 0}], "briefing_id": "art-002", "briefing_title": "Liberating Aleppo", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy Liberating Aleppo Published: Wed, 12/14/2016 - 13:55 Updated: Tue, 08/08/2017 - 10:12 As pro-government forces recapture Aleppo from radical outfits, there is hope that some semblance of normalcy could return to the war-ravaged city. It is also an opportune moment to revisit the western narrative regarding the crisis Observed through the prism of history it was undoubtedly a blitzkrieg.  Within a space of about ten days, Aleppo’s eastern district that was under the occupation of myriad Islamic radical outfits wilted like a candle under enormous pressure mounted by the joint coalition of the Syrian Arab Army, Russian airforce and the Hezbullah militia. Success came to them in large increments: the area of occupation of Bashar Assad loyalists increased from 68 to 85 percent in one week and finally to 98 percent. It is at this point that the western media that had been romanticising the resistance of the Islamic radicals- including Abu Sakkar who posed in front of a camera eating the guts of a Syrian soldier- began to demand that the Western powers should step into the stop the 'genocide' of the 200,000 that had been trapped in the enclave that was under the militants control. The Russians who were lambasted in the UN General Assembly by US Ambassador Samantha Power for the pitilessness with which they killed children have agreed to a ceasefire and a controlled exit of the rebels to Idlib and other areas will take place soon. The victory in Aleppo is big for Syrian President Basher Al Assad, who would now control all large cities except Idlib. The city is also significant as it is the commercial capital of the country that was methodically ravaged by militants who dismantled factories, hardware and ferried the loot to neighbouring Turkey. Though half of Aleppo was always under the control of the government, the return of the entire city to the control of pro-government forces allows Assad to start thinking of the possibility of rebuilding his country once the war ends. Peace, though, is unlikely to return any time soon if the recapture of ancient city of Palmyra by ISIS is anything to go by. There are vested global and regional interests that don’t want stability in this country till their geo-strategic interests are met. The fall of Aleppo and the subsequent recapture of Palmyra are a manifestation of the serious jostling for influence that is taking place in Syria between West and the Syrian backers like Russia and Iran. What is really aggrav"}, {"phase_num": 3, "phase_total": 3, "title": "The Houthi Gambit and the Red Sea", "period": "2015 — present", "context": "Iran's relationship with Yemen's Houthi movement evolved from ideological sympathy to active proxy partnership as the Saudi-led intervention created the perfect conditions for Iranian involvement. The Houthis receive Iranian missiles, drones, and military expertise. By 2019, they attack Saudi Aramco's Abqaiq facility, briefly cutting global oil supply by 5%. In 2023-24, amid the Gaza war, Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping force global cargo rerouting — adding billions to supply chain costs and demonstrating that Iran's cheapest proxy now has global economic reach.", "question": "The Houthis have disrupted global shipping from Yemen. Iran has gained enormous leverage at relatively low cost. How does the world respond?", "briefing_key": "the yemen quagmire", "choices": [{"label": "Western naval presence in the Red Sea can deter and degrade Houthi capabilities", "outcome_title": "Whack-a-Mole at Sea", "outcome": "Operation Prosperity Guardian deploys Western naval assets. US and UK strikes degrade Houthi missile and drone stockpiles. The Houthis adapt — dispersing assets, rebuilding from Iranian supplies. Shipping diversion continues for months. The lesson: naval power can reduce but not eliminate the threat of a land-based missile force with a determined patron.", "score": 0}, {"label": "Address the root cause — a Gaza ceasefire removes the Houthis' political justification", "outcome_title": "The Political Solution", "outcome": "The Houthis have framed their Red Sea campaign as solidarity with Palestinians in Gaza. A ceasefire removes this political cover. But the Houthis' targeting capability, once demonstrated globally, cannot be forgotten. Even a ceasefire leaves the world knowing a Yemeni militia can disrupt global shipping with Iranian backing.", "score": 1}, {"label": "Iran has demonstrated its proxy model works at global scale — this changes deterrence calculus permanently", "outcome_title": "The New Normal", "outcome": "The Houthi Red Sea campaign marks a strategic inflection: Iran can now project economic disruption globally through proxies at minimal direct cost. The Abqaiq attack, the Houthi Red Sea campaign, and Hezbollah's northern Israel front together constitute an unprecedented level of coercive capacity for a sanctioned state. The deterrence architecture of the 21st century has shifted.", "score": 0}], "briefing_id": "art-014", "briefing_title": "The Yemen Quagmire", "briefing_text": "Foreign Policy The Yemen Quagmire Published: Thu, 03/12/2015 - 10:04 Updated: Tue, 06/30/2015 - 09:50 Saudi Arabia has been using the rise of Iran backed Houthi tribals in Yemen as an excuse to press for foreign intervention Sabika Zehra Delhi Tales of the stealthy escape of Yemen’s President, Abdu Rabbu Mansour Hadi, shrouded in a woman’s chador (veil) from the house arrest imposed by Houthi rebels have been trending in the streets of Sanaa. Others say that he passed through numerous Houthi checkpoints in a container full of frozen meat. However, as intriguing as the story of his escape from the heavily guarded city may be, Hadi’s arrival in his hometown in the southern port of Aden last month marks a new chapter in a protracted political crisis. With Hadi retracting the resignation he made as a virtual prisoner of the rebels and branding all of the measures they have since adopted as “null and illegitimate,” the UN-sponsored reconciliation talks came to an abrupt standstill. As UN Secretary-General Ban Ki Moon recapitulated in his address to the Security Council, that political turmoil in Yemen is leading to an impending humanitarian disaster. Two-thirds of the population, including more than 840,000 acutely malnourished children, is in dire need of humanitarian aid. Grant Pritchard, head of advocacy from Oxfam in Sanaa, referred to the Yemeni humanitarian situation as a “forgotten crisis”. Over 10 million people in Yemen need food assistance, while about 13 million have no access to safe water, sanitation or adequate healthcare. With no foreseeable resolution in the near future, turmoil in Yemen has been a matter of serious concern to Westerners and Middle Easterners, alike. Southern Yemen has been the breeding ground and centre of the Al-Qaeda in Arabian Peninsula (AQAP), which is responsible for several terrorist actions in the West, including the Charlie Hebdo massacre. The Yemeni government had been cooperative with the United States in its war on terror and against the AQAP. The recent political flux and resulting power vacuum has created a scenario conducive for the growth and activities of the terrorist groups. Yemen also captures Western interests because of its strategic location. The Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden are important shipping lanes for oil containers travelling from the region to the West. Chaos in Yemen endangers oil trade in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden with increased piracy. The alleged ties between the Houthi rebels and Iran a"}], "outcome_title": "The Shadow That Grows", "outcome_text": "Iran's proxy network — built over four decades from Hezbollah's founding in 1982 to the Houthi Red Sea campaign of 2023-24 — represents the most sophisticated and durable example of asymmetric power projection by a non-nuclear state in modern history. The network costs Iran dearly in money and international isolation. But it gives Tehran deterrence, strategic depth, and coercive reach that no amount of conventional military spending could buy. The articles in this archive document this shadow war from multiple angles — from Sanjay Kapoor's geopolitical analysis to the on-the-ground reporting on what Iran's involvement actually looks like in Syria and Yemen. The Axis of Resistance is also an axis of perpetual, expensive, indeterminate conflict."}];
